/* =====================================================
   FUGA MYTHICA — ROOT_STORIES batch 4 (shared Latin/Greek roots) + aliases
   ===================================================== */
Object.assign(ROOT_STORIES.alias, { ten2: "tend", mett: "mitt", fac2: "fac", tent: "ten", spec2: "spec", duc2: "duc", pend2: "pend", plic2: "plic", sequ2: "sequ", mit2: "mitt", vid2: "vid", dat2: "don", ced2: "ced", cur3: "curr", form2: "form", secu: "sequ", ri: "re", par3: "par", tempor: "temp", astr: "stell", nat: "nat2", nasc: "nat2", reg2: "reg", uni: "un" });
Object.assign(ROOT_STORIES.shared, {
  agr: ["ager(田,属格 agri)来自印欧语 *h₂eǵros(牧场——\"驱赶牲畜的地方\",与 agere 驱赶同根),同根有希腊语 agros、梵语 ajra、英语 acre(古义任何田地,后指一天犁的量)、德语 Acker。罗马的 ager publicus(公地)分配问题引发了格拉古兄弟的改革(前 133 年);agri-cola 耕田者(agriculture 耕田术),agrarius 土地的(agrarian 土地法),per-egrinus 是\"穿过田野来的\"——外乡人(peregrine 游隼、pilgrim 朝圣者都是它),agronomy 希腊线的田地管理,agora 是希腊的市集(\"聚集处\",同根),Agricola 是塔西佗岳父的名字。",
    "ager (a field, genitive agri) comes from PIE *h₂eǵros (pasture — \"where cattle are driven\", kin to agere), shared with Greek agros, Sanskrit ajra, English acre (any field, later a day's ploughing), German Acker. The division of Rome's ager publicus, the public land, drove the Gracchan reforms of 133 BC; an agri-cola tills the field (agriculture), agrarius of the land (agrarian laws), per-egrinus one who comes across the fields — a foreigner (the peregrine falcon and the pilgrim are both this word), agronomy the Greek line's field-management, the agora the Greek gathering-place (same root), Agricola the name of Tacitus' father-in-law."],
  fabr: ["faber(工匠)来自印欧语 *dʰabʰ-(装配、合适),同根有亚美尼亚语 darbin(铁匠)、哥特语 gadaban(适合)、古教会斯拉夫语 dobrŭ(好)。罗马的 faber 是金属匠、木匠;fabrica 是作坊、也是手艺(fabric 先指建筑物,17 世纪转为织物;forge 是 fabrica 经法语 forge 的磨损),fabricare 制造(fabricate 后指捏造),faber 的形容词 fabrilis,Fabius 氏族是\"工匠家\",fabulous 与此无关(fabula)。Faber est suae quisque fortunae——人人都是自己命运的工匠(阿庇乌斯·克劳狄乌斯)。",
    "faber (a craftsman) comes from PIE *dʰabʰ- (to fit together), shared with Armenian darbin (smith), Gothic gadaban (to fit), Old Church Slavonic dobrŭ (good). The Roman faber was a smith or carpenter; a fabrica was his workshop and his craft (fabric meant a building before it meant cloth in the 17th century; forge is fabrica worn down through French), fabricare to make (fabricate later to invent falsely), the gens Fabia the craftsmen's clan; fabulous is unrelated (fabula). Faber est suae quisque fortunae — every man is the maker of his own fortune (Appius Claudius)."],
  lingu: ["lingua(舌、语言)来自印欧语 *dn̥ǵʰwéh₂s(舌),同根有英语 tongue、德语 Zunge、梵语 jihvā——古拉丁语是 dingua,d 变成 l 是受 lingere(舔)影响。罗马人 lingua 既是舌头也是语言(lingua Latina);lingu-alis 舌的(lingual),bi-linguis 双舌的——双语(bilingual),linguista 是 16 世纪的\"语言学家\",lingua franca 是地中海港口的\"法兰克语\"——通用混合语,language 是 lingua 经法语 langage,lingo 是 lingua 的俚语形,linguine 是意大利语\"小舌头\"面条,lingerie 与此无关(linum)。",
    "lingua (tongue, language) comes from PIE *dn̥ǵʰwéh₂s (tongue), shared with English tongue, German Zunge, Sanskrit jihvā — Old Latin had dingua, and the d became l by association with lingere (to lick). Roman lingua was the tongue and the language (lingua Latina); lingu-alis of the tongue (lingual), bi-linguis two-tongued (bilingual), linguista a 16th-century language expert, lingua franca the \"Frankish tongue\" of Mediterranean ports — a trade pidgin, language is lingua through French langage, lingo its slang form, linguine Italian little tongues of pasta; lingerie is unrelated (linum)."],
  misc: ["miscere(混合)来自印欧语 *meiḱ-(混),同根有希腊语 misgein、mignynai(混)、英语 mix(经拉丁语 mixtus)、mash、德语 mischen、梵语 miśra(混合的)。罗马人 miscere 兑酒(古人喝酒必兑水);mixtus 混合的(mixture 混合物、mix 动词 16 世纪从 mixt 倒推),mis-cellaneus 各种混杂的(miscellaneous),pro-miscuus 不分彼此混杂的(promiscuous 19 世纪转为性关系),mestizo 是西班牙语 mixticius 混血,meddle 是 miscere 经法语 mesler(先指混合,后指插手),medley 混成曲,mélange 法语混合,mixer、admixture。",
    "miscere (to mix) comes from PIE *meiḱ- (to mix), shared with Greek misgein and mignynai, English mix (via Latin mixtus) and mash, German mischen, Sanskrit miśra (mixed). Romans miscere wine with water (the ancients never drank it neat); mixtus mixed (mixture; the verb mix was back-formed from mixt in the 16th century), mis-cellaneus of all sorts (miscellaneous), pro-miscuus mixed without distinction (promiscuous, sexual only since the 19th century), mestizo Spanish mixticius, of mixed blood, meddle is miscere through French mesler (to mix, then to interfere), medley, mélange, mixer, admixture."],
  noc: ["nocere(伤害)来自印欧语 *neḱ-(消亡、死),同根有 nex(暴死)、necare(杀死)、希腊语 nekros(尸体——necropolis、necrosis)、nektar(\"胜过死亡\"的神饮)、梵语 naśyati(消失)。罗马人 nocere 是加害;in-nocens 是\"不伤人的\"——无辜(innocent),noxius 有害的(noxious;obnoxious 本义\"易受害的\",后转为可憎),noxa 损害,in-nocuus 无害(innocuous),nuisance 是 nocentia 经法语——妨害,internecine 是 inter-necare 互相杀戮,pernicious 是 per-nicies 彻底毁灭,nectar 与 nekros 同根。",
    "nocere (to harm) comes from PIE *neḱ- (to perish, die), shared with nex (violent death), necare (to kill), Greek nekros (a corpse — necropolis, necrosis), nektar (the drink that overcomes death), Sanskrit naśyati (vanishes). Romans nocere to injure; in-nocens \"not harming\" — innocent, noxius harmful (noxious; obnoxious meant exposed to harm before it meant hateful), noxa damage, in-nocuus harmless (innocuous), nuisance is nocentia through French — an injury, internecine is inter-necare, mutual slaughter, pernicious per-nicies, utter ruin, and nectar shares its root with nekros."],
  norm: ["norma(木匠的直角尺)可能是希腊语 gnōmōn(日晷指针、直角尺)经伊特鲁里亚语借入的变形。罗马人的 norma 是量直角的工具,引申为准则;normalis 是\"成直角的\"——正常义是 19 世纪才从\"符合标准\"来的(normal school 师范学校是 école normale 的直译,\"树立标准的学校\",1794),ab-normis 偏离尺子(abnormal),e-normis 超出尺子——巨大(enormous),norm 1820 年代,normative 规范的,normalize,paranormal 超常。",
    "norma (a carpenter's square) is probably Greek gnōmōn (a sundial pointer, a set-square) reshaped through Etruscan. The Roman norma measured right angles, hence a rule; normalis was \"at right angles\" — the sense of ordinary came only in the 19th century from \"conforming to the standard\" (a normal school translates école normale, the school that sets the standard, 1794), ab-normis off the square (abnormal), e-normis beyond the square — huge (enormous), norm 1820s, normative, normalize, paranormal."],
  nutr: ["nutrire(喂养)来自印欧语 *(s)neh₂u-(流、哺乳)存疑,同根的可能有 nurus(儿媳)不,而是 nato(游泳)、希腊语 nāō(流)。罗马的 nutrix 是乳母(nurse 是它经法语 norrice——先指乳母,19 世纪才指护士),nutrimentum 营养(nutriment),nutritio 哺育(nutrition),nutrire 经法语 nourrir 给了 nourish、nourishment,nurture 是 nutritura 经法语,nutrient、nutritious 17 世纪,nuts 与此无关。",
    "nutrire (to nourish) may come from PIE *(s)neh₂u- (to flow, suckle), with nato (to swim) and Greek nāō (to flow) as possible cousins. The Roman nutrix was the wet-nurse (nurse is nutrix through French norrice — a wet-nurse first, a hospital nurse only in the 19th century), nutrimentum nourishment (nutriment), nutritio feeding (nutrition), nutrire through French nourrir gave nourish and nourishment, nurture is nutritura through French, nutrient and nutritious 17th-century."],
  cal: ["calere(热)来自印欧语 *ḱelh₁-(热),同根有梵语 śarad(秋——\"热季\")、立陶宛语 šilti(变暖)、威尔士语 clyd(暖)。罗马人 calor 是热(caloric、calorie 是 1824 年法国的热量单位),calidus 热的(法语 chaud、意大利语 caldo、西班牙语 caliente 都是它),caldarium 罗马浴场的热水室,cauldron 是 caldarium 经法语,chafe 是 calefacere(使热)经法语——先指加热后指磨擦,chowder 是法语 chaudière(锅),chauffeur 是法语\"司炉\"——蒸汽车的加热者,scald 是 ex-caldare 烫,nonchalant 是\"不发热\"——满不在乎。",
    "calere (to be hot) comes from PIE *ḱelh₁- (warm), shared with Sanskrit śarad (autumn — the hot season), Lithuanian šilti (to warm), Welsh clyd (warm). Roman calor is heat (caloric; the calorie was France's 1824 heat unit), calidus hot (French chaud, Italian caldo, Spanish caliente), the caldarium the hot room of the baths, cauldron caldarium through French, chafe calefacere (to make warm) through French — to heat, then to rub, chowder French chaudière (a pot), chauffeur the French \"stoker\" of a steam car, scald ex-caldare, nonchalant \"not heating up\" — unconcerned."],
  culp: ["culpa(过失、责任)是拉丁语独有的词,词源不明。罗马法把 culpa(过失)与 dolus(故意)分开——今天大陆法仍如此;mea culpa(我的过错)是弥撒忏悔词里捶胸时念的,culpare 责备,culpabilis 有罪的(culpable),ex-culpare 开脱(exculpate),in-culpare 归罪(inculpate),culprit 是 1670 年代英国法庭的缩略:检察官答辩 cul. prit(culpable, prêt——\"有罪,准备证明\"),被误解成对被告的称呼。",
    "culpa (fault, blame) is a purely Latin word of unknown origin. Roman law separated culpa (negligence) from dolus (intent) — civil-law systems still do; mea culpa is beaten on the breast in the Confiteor of the Mass, culpare to blame, culpabilis blameworthy (culpable), ex-culpare to clear (exculpate), in-culpare to charge (inculpate), and culprit is a 1670s English courtroom abbreviation: the prosecutor's cul. prit (culpable, prêt — guilty, and ready to prove it) misread as a name for the accused."],
  matur: ["maturus(成熟、及时)来自印欧语 *meh₂-(好、及时),同根有 mane(早晨——\"好时辰\")、Matuta(晨曦女神)、manus(好的,古义)。罗马人 maturus 是果实熟了、也是\"时候到了\";maturare 使成熟(mature),maturitas 成熟(maturity),prae-maturus 熟得太早(premature),im-maturus 未熟,matinée 是法语\"早晨\"——后指日场演出,matins 晨祷,manana 与此无关(mane 是),Matuta 的节日 Matralia。",
    "maturus (ripe, timely) comes from PIE *meh₂- (good, timely), shared with mane (morning — the good hour), Matuta (the dawn goddess), and manus in its old sense of good. Roman maturus was fruit ripened and the moment come; maturare to ripen (mature), maturitas ripeness (maturity), prae-maturus ripe too soon (premature), im-maturus unripe, matinée French for morning — later an afternoon show, matins the morning office; Spanish mañana descends from mane; Matuta's festival was the Matralia."],
  noct: ["nox(夜,属格 noctis)是印欧语 *nókʷts:希腊语 nyx、梵语 nakt-、英语 night、德语 Nacht、俄语 noch'。罗马人的 noctu 是夜里,nocturnus 夜间的(nocturnal;nocturne 是 1810 年代菲尔德为夜曲起的名),noct-ua 猫头鹰(夜鸟),aequi-noctium 昼夜平分(equinox),per-noctare 过夜,noct-ambulus 夜游者,nyktalopia 是希腊线的夜盲。",
    "nox (night, genitive noctis) is Indo-European *nókʷts: Greek nyx, Sanskrit nakt-, English night, German Nacht, Russian noch'. Roman noctu is by night, nocturnus of the night (nocturnal; the nocturne was Field's name for a night-piece in the 1810s), noct-ua the owl (night-bird), aequi-noctium equal night (equinox), per-noctare to spend the night, noct-ambulus a night-walker, nyktalopia the Greek line's night-blindness."],
  pil: ["拉丁语有三个 pil-。pila(球)——罗马人的球戏 pila,pilula 小球——药丸(pill),pilot 与此无关(希腊语 pēdon 桨);pila(柱、墩)——pilaster 壁柱(意大利语 pilastro),pile 桩,pillar 经法语 piler,compile 是 com-pilare\"堆到一起\"——本义抢劫;pilus(毛)——pilosus 多毛,depilate 脱毛,caterpillar 是法语 chatepelose\"毛猫\",pluck 无关。三者词源都不明,可能有两个是同一个\"堆\"。",
    "Latin has three pil-. pila (a ball) — the Roman ball game was pila, a pilula a little ball — a pill (pilot is unrelated, Greek pēdon, an oar); pila (a pillar, a pier) — pilaster (Italian pilastro), pile, pillar through French piler, compile is com-pilare, \"to heap together\" — originally to plunder; pilus (a hair) — pilosus hairy, depilate, caterpillar French chatepelose, \"hairy cat\". All three are of obscure origin; two may be one word for a heap."],
  pred: ["praeda(战利品、猎物)是 prae-heda(\"先抓到手的\")的缩合,与 prehendere(抓)同根。罗马军队的 praeda 是分给士兵的掠夺物;praedari 掠夺,praedator 掠夺者(predator 1920 年代才用于动物),praedatio 掠夺(depredation),prey 是 praeda 经法语 preie,predatory 掠夺性的,praedium 田产是另一个词。",
    "praeda (booty, prey) is prae-heda, \"what is seized first\", contracted, kin to prehendere (to grasp). A Roman army's praeda was the plunder shared among the soldiers; praedari to plunder, praedator a plunderer (predator was applied to animals only in the 1920s), praedatio plundering (depredation), prey is praeda through French preie, predatory; praedium, an estate, is another word."],
  leg4: ["lex(法律,属格 legis)可能来自 legere(收集、读)——法律是\"收集起来读出的条文\"(《十二表法》就是刻在板上公开宣读的),或来自 *leǵ-(约定)。罗马的 lex 是公民大会通过的成文法(与 ius 抽象的\"法权\"相对);legalis 法律的(legal、loyal 是它经法语 leial——守法即忠诚),legitimus 合法的(legitimate),legis-lator 提出法律的人,privi-legium 特权,col-lega 是\"按同一法律选出的同僚\"(colleague、college 是同僚团体),legatus 是受法律委派的使节(legate、legacy 是遗嘱委派——遗产),relegate 依法放逐,delegate 委派。",
    "lex (law, genitive legis) may come from legere (to gather, read) — a law was a text gathered and read out (the Twelve Tables were carved and proclaimed) — or from *leǵ- (to bind by agreement). Roman lex was statute passed by the assembly (as against ius, abstract right); legalis legal (loyal is legalis through French leial — the law-abiding man is faithful), legitimus lawful (legitimate), a legis-lator a proposer of laws, privi-legium a private law, col-lega one chosen under the same law (colleague; a college is a body of colleagues), legatus one sent under law (legate; a legacy is a bequest by will), relegate to banish by law, delegate to depute."],
  oma: ["希腊语后缀 -ōma 本义\"…的结果、…的东西\"(如 sōma 身体、aiōma? 不,如 stoma 口);医学把它专用于\"肿块\":carcin-ōma 是希波克拉底的\"螃蟹肿\"(癌——肿瘤周围的血管像蟹脚),sarc-ōma 肉瘤,lymph-oma 淋巴瘤,melan-oma 黑色瘤(1830 年代),glauc-ōma 是希腊语\"蓝灰色眼\"(青光眼,古人指瞳孔变色),ather-oma 粥样瘤,hemat-oma 血肿,fibr-oma。carcinoma 里的 karkinos(螃蟹)与拉丁语 cancer 同根——cancer 就是拉丁语的螃蟹。",
    "The Greek suffix -ōma means \"the result of, the thing produced\" (as in sōma body, stoma mouth); medicine reserved it for swellings: carcin-ōma is Hippocrates' crab-tumour (the vessels round a growth looked like a crab's legs), sarc-ōma a flesh-tumour, lymph-oma, melan-oma the black tumour (1830s), glauc-ōma Greek for a grey-blue eye (the ancients meant the discoloured pupil), ather-oma the gruel-tumour, hemat-oma a blood-swelling, fibr-oma. The karkinos of carcinoma is kin to Latin cancer — the crab."],
  ject: ["iacere(扔)的分词 iactus 在复合词里变 -iectus/-ject-,是拉丁语最多产的词干之一(见 iacio):ob-iectum 是\"扔到前面\"——摆在面前的东西(object,经院哲学的\"对象\";objection 反对是扔过去的话),sub-iectum 是\"扔到下面\"——托底的东西(subject 先指主体、后指臣民、再指主题),pro-iectum 向前扔(project 计划;projectile 抛射体),in-iectio 注射,e-iectio 抛出(eject),re-iectio 拒绝(reject),de-iectus 扔下的——沮丧(dejected),ad-iectivum 是\"加上去的词\"(adjective),con-iectura 是\"一起扔\"——推测(conjecture),traiectoria 弹道(trajectory),jet 是 iactare 经法语 jeter(扔)——喷射,jetty 突堤,jettison 抛货,jut 突出。",
    "The participle iactus of iacere (to throw) becomes -iectus/-ject- in compounds, one of Latin's most productive stems (see iacio): ob-iectum is what is thrown before you — an object (the schoolmen's object of thought; an objection is words thrown against), sub-iectum what is thrown beneath — the underlying thing (subject: first the substance, then the king's subject, then a topic), pro-iectum thrown forward (a project; a projectile), in-iectio injection, e-iectio ejection, re-iectio rejection, de-iectus cast down (dejected), ad-iectivum the word thrown on (adjective), con-iectura a throwing together — conjecture, traiectoria trajectory, jet is iactare through French jeter — a spurt, jetty, jettison, jut."],
  loc: ["locus(地方)是古拉丁语 stlocus,词源不明——可能与 *stel-(放置,与 stall 同根)有关。罗马人的 locus 是位置、也是文章里的\"一处\"(loci communes 常见论题——commonplace 由此);localis 地方的(local 1690 年代指地方政府),locare 放置、出租(locate 定位是 1650 年代美洲测量用语),locatio 出租(lease 与此无关),al-locare 分配(allocate),col-locare 安放(collocate),dis-locare 移位(dislocate),locus 复数 loci,lieu 是法语的地方(in lieu of),lieutenant 是\"代替者\"(占位的人),locomotive 是\"从一地移动\"(1810 年代),couch 是 collocare 经法语 coucher(躺下)——沙发。",
    "locus (a place) was Old Latin stlocus, of uncertain origin — perhaps *stel- (to set, kin to stall). Roman locus was a position and a passage in a text (loci communes, common topics — whence commonplace); localis of a place (local government 1690s), locare to place, to let out (locate in the 1650s was American surveyors' usage), locatio a letting, al-locare to assign (allocate), col-locare to set together (collocate), dis-locare to displace (dislocate), lieu French for place (in lieu of), a lieutenant a place-holder, a locomotive moves from place to place (1810s), couch is collocare through French coucher, to lay down."],
  serv: ["servare(保存、看守)与 servus(奴隶)是否同根有争议:servare 来自印欧语 *ser-(看守,与阿维斯陀语 haraiti 守护同根),servus 可能是伊特鲁里亚语借词。罗马法里 servus 是奴隶,servire 是做奴隶——服侍(serve 经法语 servir,service 是 servitium 奴役,后指教会礼拜和公共服务,servant 侍者,sergeant 是 serviens 服务的人,serf 农奴,servile 卑屈的);servare 保存:con-servare(conserve、conservative 1830 年代政治义),prae-servare(preserve),re-servare(reserve),ob-servare 看守——遵守、观察(observe),dessert 是 des-servir\"撤桌\"后上的。",
    "Whether servare (to keep, guard) and servus (slave) share a root is disputed: servare comes from PIE *ser- (to guard, kin to Avestan haraiti), while servus may be an Etruscan loan. In Roman law a servus is a slave, servire to be a slave — to serve (through French servir; service was servitium, slavery, later church worship and public service, servant, sergeant a serviens, one who serves, serf, servile); servare to keep: con-servare (conserve; conservative in politics from the 1830s), prae-servare (preserve), re-servare (reserve), ob-servare to keep watch — to obey, to observe, and dessert is what comes after des-servir, clearing the table."],
  mal: ["malus(坏)来自印欧语 *mel-(坏、假)存疑,同根的可能有希腊语 meleos(徒劳)、阿维斯陀语 mairya(欺诈)。罗马人 male 是副词\"坏\";mal-ignus 是\"生性坏的\"(malignant),male-dicere 咒骂(malediction),male-ficium 恶行(maleficent),male-volens 恶意(malevolent),malitia 恶意(malice),malum 坏事、也是苹果(另一个词,希腊语 mēlon——所以中世纪把禁果当成苹果是双关),malady 是 male habitus(状态坏)经法语 maladie,malaise 法语\"不舒服\",malaria 是意大利语 mal'aria\"坏空气\"(18 世纪以为沼气致病),dismal 是 dies mali(凶日)经法语,malfunction 1928。",
    "malus (bad) may come from PIE *mel- (bad, false), with Greek meleos (futile) and Avestan mairya (deceitful) as possible cousins. Roman male is the adverb badly; mal-ignus bad by nature (malignant), male-dicere to curse (malediction), male-ficium evil-doing (maleficent), male-volens ill-wishing (malevolent), malitia ill will (malice), malum a bad thing and also an apple (a different word, Greek mēlon — the medieval apple in Eden is a pun), malady is male habitus, badly kept, through French maladie, malaise French for discomfort, malaria Italian mal'aria, bad air (the 18th century blamed marsh vapours), dismal dies mali, unlucky days, through French, malfunction 1928."],
  inter: ["inter(在…之间)来自印欧语 *enter(在里面、之间),是 in 的比较级形式;同根有梵语 antar、古爱尔兰语 eter、德语 unter(在…之下/之间——unter uns 我们之间)、英语 under。罗马人 inter-esse 是\"在其间\"——有关系(interest),inter-rogare 问,inter-venire 介入(intervene),inter-pretari 解释(interpret),inter-vallum 是营地壁垒之间的空地(interval),inter-regnum 两王之间的空位期,inter-nus 内部的(internal;intern 是关在里面的人、实习生),intra 在内,interim 其间,intimus 是 inter 的最高级——最里面的(intimate),enter 是 intrare 经法语,entrails 是 interanea(内部器官)。",
    "inter (between) comes from PIE *enter (within, between), a comparative of in; cognates are Sanskrit antar, Old Irish eter, German unter (under/among — unter uns, between ourselves), English under. Roman inter-esse is to be between — to matter (interest), inter-rogare to ask, inter-venire to come between (intervene), inter-pretari to explain (interpret), inter-vallum the space between the camp's ramparts (interval), inter-regnum the gap between kings, inter-nus inward (internal; an intern is one kept inside, a trainee), intra within, interim meanwhile, intimus the superlative of inter — innermost (intimate), enter is intrare through French, entrails are interanea, the inner parts."],
  trans: ["trans(越过)可能是 *trā-(穿过)的分词形式(与 terminus 同根),同根有梵语 tiras(穿过)、古爱尔兰语 tar、英语 through、德语 durch。罗马人 trans-ire 越过(transit、transient 过路的),trans-ferre 搬过去(transfer),trans-latum 是它的分词——翻译(translate),trans-portare 运过(transport),trans-formare 变形,trans-scribere 抄写(transcribe),trans-actio 交易,trans-parens 透光的(transparent),trans-spirare 透过皮肤呼吸(transpire 后误用为\"发生\"),trans-Alpinus 阿尔卑斯以北的,tra-dere 交出(tradition、treason、traitor 都是 trans+dare),traverse 横过,trespass 越界,tres- 在法语里是 très(\"超过\"——很)。",
    "trans (across) is probably an old participle of *trā- (to cross, kin to terminus), shared with Sanskrit tiras (across), Old Irish tar, English through, German durch. Romans trans-ire to go across (transit; transient passing through), trans-ferre to carry across (transfer), trans-latum its participle — to translate, trans-portare (transport), trans-formare (transform), trans-scribere (transcribe), trans-actio (transaction), trans-parens letting light through (transparent), trans-spirare to breathe out through the skin (transpire, later misused for \"happen\"), trans-Alpinus beyond the Alps, tra-dere to hand across (tradition, treason and traitor are all trans + dare), traverse, trespass, and French très, \"very\", is trans — beyond."],
  chron: ["希腊语 khronos(时间)词源不明;神话把它与 Kronos(克洛诺斯,宙斯之父)混同——吞吃子女的时间。khronikos 时间的(chronic 病是\"拖时间的\",1600 年代),khronika 编年记(chronicle),chrono-logia 时间之学(chronology 1590 年代),ana-chronismos 时间错位(anachronism),syn-chronos 同时(synchronize),chrono-meter 1714 年为航海精密钟造的词,chronograph 计时器,diachronic 历时的(索绪尔 1916)。",
    "Greek khronos (time) is of unknown origin; myth confused it with Kronos, Zeus' father, the Titan who devoured his children — time that eats its own. khronikos of time (a chronic illness drags on, 1600s), khronika annals (chronicle), chrono-logia the science of time (chronology 1590s), ana-chronismos a displacement in time (anachronism), syn-chronos at the same time (synchronize), chrono-meter coined in 1714 for the marine timekeeper, chronograph, diachronic (Saussure 1916)."],
  plaud: ["plaudere(拍手)是拉丁语独有的拟声词。罗马喜剧的最后一句台词是 Plaudite!(鼓掌吧);奥古斯都临终问朋友:\"我演得好吗?那就鼓掌吧。\"ap-plaudere 朝着拍手(applaud、applause),ex-plaudere 是\"拍手把演员轰下台\"——explode 到 17 世纪都指嘘下台,后转为爆炸(1790 年代),plausibilis 是\"值得鼓掌的\"——plausible 从\"可喝彩\"变成\"似乎可信\"(16 世纪),plaudit 是 plaudite 的英语缩写。",
    "plaudere (to clap) is a purely Latin, onomatopoeic word. The last line of a Roman comedy was Plaudite! — applaud; the dying Augustus asked his friends whether he had played his part well, and if so to clap. ap-plaudere to clap toward (applaud, applause), ex-plaudere to clap an actor off the stage — explode meant to hoot off until the 17th century and only became bursting in the 1790s, plausibilis worthy of applause — plausible went from \"deserving cheers\" to \"seemingly true\" in the 16th century, plaudit is plaudite clipped in English."],
  rig: ["rigere(僵硬、冻硬)来自印欧语 *reig-(伸直、硬),同根有 frigus(冷)有争议、希腊语 rhigos(寒冷)、英语 无。罗马人 rigidus 是僵硬的(rigid),rigor 僵硬——严厉(rigour;rigor mortis 尸僵 1830 年代),rigorosus 严格(rigorous),rigidity。另一个 rigare(灌溉)不同源:ir-rigare 引水入田(irrigate),rigation。",
    "rigere (to be stiff, frozen) comes from PIE *reig- (to stretch out, stiffen), shared with Greek rhigos (cold); a link with frigus is disputed. Roman rigidus is stiff (rigid), rigor stiffness — severity (rigour; rigor mortis 1830s), rigorosus strict (rigorous), rigidity. rigare (to water) is a different word: ir-rigare to lead water into the field (irrigate)."],
  nomin: ["nomen(名,属格 nominis)是印欧语 *h₁nómn̥:希腊语 onoma、梵语 nāman、英语 name、德语 Name、俄语 imya。罗马人有三名(tria nomina):praenomen 名、nomen 氏族名、cognomen 家族名;nominare 命名、提名(nominate),nominalis 名义上的(nominal),nomenclatura 是奴隶 nomenclator 在竞选时向主人报路人名字的本事——命名法,de-nominare 命名(denomination 教派、面额),ig-nominia 失名(ignominy),nomen 经法语 nom 给了 noun(名词)和 renown(re-nommer 再叫——名声),nominative 主格,misnomer 错名,pronoun 代名。",
    "nomen (name, genitive nominis) is Indo-European *h₁nómn̥: Greek onoma, Sanskrit nāman, English name, German Name, Russian imya. A Roman had tria nomina: the praenomen, the nomen of his gens and the cognomen of his family; nominare to name, to propose (nominate), nominalis in name only (nominal), nomenclatura the skill of the nomenclator, the slave who whispered passers-by's names to a canvassing master — a system of naming, de-nominare to name (denomination — a sect, a coin's value), ig-nominia loss of name (ignominy), nomen through French nom gave noun and renown (re-nommer, to name again — fame), nominative, misnomer, pronoun."],
  homo: ["希腊语 homos(相同)来自印欧语 *somos(同一个),同根有英语 same(经古挪威语)、梵语 sama、拉丁语 similis(相似)、simul(同时)。homo-geneia 同类(homogeneous),homo-nymos 同名(homonym),homo-logos 相应的(homologous),homo-sexual 是 1869 年克特贝尼用希腊拉丁混造的词,homoio-pathēs 是哈内曼 1796 年的\"同样受苦\"——顺势疗法(homeopathy),homoiousios/homoousios(相似/同一本质)是 325 年尼西亚会议争论的一个字母 i,homeostasis 1926 年坎农造。拉丁语的 homo(人)是另一个词。",
    "Greek homos (same) comes from PIE *somos (one and the same), shared with English same (via Old Norse), Sanskrit sama, Latin similis (like) and simul (at the same time). homo-geneia of one kind (homogeneous), homo-nymos same-named (homonym), homo-logos agreeing (homologous), homosexual Kertbeny's 1869 Greek-Latin hybrid, homoio-pathēs Hahnemann's 1796 \"suffering alike\" — homeopathy, homoiousios versus homoousios (of like versus the same substance) the single iota fought over at Nicaea in 325, homeostasis Cannon's coinage of 1926. Latin homo (a human) is a different word."],
  onym: ["希腊语 onoma(名)在复合词里变成 -ōnymos:syn-ōnymon 同义词(synonym,\"一起的名\"),ant-ōnymon 反义词(antonym 1867),hom-ōnymon 同名(homonym),an-ōnymos 无名(anonymous),pseud-ōnymos 假名(pseudonym),epōnymos 是\"给出名字的\"——雅典每年以首席执政官命名(eponymous;eponym 1846),acr-onym 首字母词(1943),topo-nym 地名,metonymy 换名(修辞),cryptonym 代号,patronymic 父名。onoma 与拉丁语 nomen、英语 name 同根。",
    "Greek onoma (a name) becomes -ōnymos in compounds: syn-ōnymon a name together (synonym), ant-ōnymon a name against (antonym 1867), hom-ōnymon same-name (homonym), an-ōnymos nameless (anonymous), pseud-ōnymos false-named (pseudonym), epōnymos the name-giver — Athens named each year after its chief archon (eponymous; eponym 1846), acr-onym a tip-name (1943), topo-nym a place-name, metonymy a change of name (rhetoric), cryptonym a hidden name, patronymic a father-name. onoma is cognate with Latin nomen and English name."],
  syn: ["希腊语 syn(一起)来自印欧语 *ksun(与)存疑,与拉丁语 cum 无关(cum 是 *kom)。在复合词里 syn- 随后面的辅音变:sym- 在 b/p/m 前(sym-pathy 同感、sym-phony 同声、sym-bol 拼合的信物、sym-metry 同尺度),syl- 在 l 前(syl-lable 拼在一起的音、syl-logism 合起来推理),sy- 在 s 前(sy-stem 站在一起、sy-zygy 同轭)。syn-agōgē 集会(synagogue),syn-odos 会议(synod),syn-thesis 合成,syn-tax 排在一起——句法,syn-chronos 同时,syn-drome 一起跑来的症状,syn-ergeia 协同(synergy),syn-opsis 一起看,syn-onym 同义。",
    "Greek syn (together) may come from PIE *ksun (with); it is unrelated to Latin cum (which is *kom). In compounds syn- changes with the following consonant: sym- before b/p/m (sym-pathy feeling with, sym-phony sounding together, sym-bol a token matched together, sym-metry one measure), syl- before l (syl-lable sounds taken together, syl-logism reasoning together), sy- before s (sy-stem standing together, sy-zygy yoked together). syn-agōgē a gathering (synagogue), syn-odos a meeting (synod), syn-thesis a putting together, syn-tax an arranging together — sentence order, syn-chronos same-time, syn-drome symptoms running together, syn-ergeia working together (synergy), syn-opsis a seeing together, syn-onym."],
  a: ["希腊语的否定前缀 a-(元音前 an-)来自印欧语 *n̥-,与拉丁语 in-、英语 un-、梵语 a-/an- 同源——同一个否定词在各语言里变了形。a-theos 无神(atheist 1570 年代),a-tomos 不可切(atom),a-nonymos 无名,an-aisthēsia 无感觉(anaesthesia),a-symmetria 不对称,a-morphos 无形(amorphous),an-archia 无统治(anarchy),a-pathēs 无感情(apathy),a-mnēsia 无记忆(amnesia),an-aemia 无血(anaemia),a-sylon 不可抢夺的地方(asylum,神庙庇护所),a-byssos 无底(abyss),a-gnōstos 不可知(agnostic),an-ekdota 未发表的(anecdote)。注意:拉丁语 ab-(离开)缩成的 a-(如 avert)是另一个前缀。",
    "The Greek negative a- (an- before vowels) comes from PIE *n̥-, one word with Latin in-, English un-, Sanskrit a-/an-. a-theos godless (atheist 1570s), a-tomos uncuttable (atom), a-nonymos nameless, an-aisthēsia without feeling (anaesthesia), a-symmetria asymmetry, a-morphos shapeless (amorphous), an-archia without a ruler (anarchy), a-pathēs without feeling (apathy), a-mnēsia without memory (amnesia), an-aemia without blood (anaemia), a-sylon a place not to be plundered (asylum — the temple refuge), a-byssos bottomless (abyss), a-gnōstos unknowable (agnostic), an-ekdota unpublished things (anecdote). Note that the a- of avert is Latin ab- (away), a different prefix."],
  lat: ["latus(边、侧,属格 lateris)来自印欧语 *stelh₂-(展开)存疑,同根的可能有 stlatta(宽船)。罗马人的 latus 是身体的一侧、军队的侧翼;lateralis 侧面的(lateral;lateral thinking 1967),col-lateralis 并排的(collateral 担保是\"旁边的\"财产,collateral damage 1940 年代),uni-lateralis 单方面的,bi-lateral 双边,quadri-lateral 四边形,e-quilateral 等边,latitude 与此无关(latus 宽的)。另一个 latus(宽的)给了 latitude 纬度(\"宽度\")、dilate 扩张、latifundia 大庄园。",
    "latus (a side, genitive lateris) may come from PIE *stelh₂- (to spread), with stlatta (a broad ship) as a possible cousin. Roman latus was the side of the body and the flank of an army; lateralis of the side (lateral; lateral thinking 1967), col-lateralis side by side (collateral is security \"alongside\" a loan; collateral damage 1940s), uni-lateralis one-sided, bi-lateral, quadri-lateral four-sided, e-quilateral equal-sided. The other latus (broad) gave latitude (breadth), dilate to widen, latifundia the great estates."],
  merg: ["mergere(浸、沉)来自印欧语 *mesg-(浸),同根有梵语 majjati(潜入)、立陶宛语 mazgóti(洗)。罗马人 mergere 把东西按进水里;e-mergere 浮出(emerge;emergency 是 1630 年代的\"突然浮现的事\"),im-mergere 浸入(immerse),sub-mergere 淹没(submerge),merger 是法律上的\"一方沉入另一方\"——合并(1720 年代),mergus 是潜鸟,de-merge 拆分,immersion 沉浸(教学法)。",
    "mergere (to dip, sink) comes from PIE *mesg- (to dip), shared with Sanskrit majjati (dives) and Lithuanian mazgóti (to wash). Romans mergere pushed things under water; e-mergere to rise out (emerge; an emergency was, in the 1630s, something suddenly rising up), im-mergere to dip in (immerse), sub-mergere to sink under (submerge), a merger is one estate sinking into another in law (1720s), mergus the diving bird, de-merge to split, immersion (as in language teaching)."],
  soci: ["socius(伙伴、盟友)来自 sequi(跟随)——\"同行的人\";印欧语 *sekʷ-。罗马的 socii 是意大利盟邦,前 91 年的\"同盟者战争\"(bellum sociale)为公民权而战;societas 是伙伴关系、合伙(society 直到 18 世纪才指\"社会\";公司 société、sociedad),socialis 伙伴的(social 1560 年代指\"合群的\",socialism 1830 年代法国),as-sociare 结伴(associate),dis-sociare 拆伙(dissociate),sociology 是孔德 1830 年代拉丁-希腊混造的\"社会学\",sociable 好交际,socio-path 1930 年。",
    "socius (companion, ally) comes from sequi (to follow) — one who goes along; PIE *sekʷ-. Rome's socii were its Italian allies, and the Social War of 91 BC (bellum sociale) was their fight for citizenship; societas was partnership (society meant the whole community only from the 18th century; French société, Spanish sociedad are also companies), socialis of companions (social meant companionable in the 1560s; socialism is 1830s French), as-sociare to join as partner (associate), dis-sociare to part company (dissociate), sociology Comte's Latin-Greek hybrid of the 1830s, sociable, socio-path 1930."],
  tox: ["希腊语 toxon(弓)是斯基泰语借词;toxikon (pharmakon) 是\"弓药\"——涂在箭上的毒。罗马人借 toxicum 指毒药;toxicus 有毒的(toxic 1660 年代),in-toxicare 是中世纪拉丁语\"下毒\"——intoxicate 到 16 世纪才转为醉酒,de-toxify 1905,toxin 1886,toxicology 1799,antitoxin 1891,toxophilite 是\"爱弓的人\"(射箭爱好者),toxophily 射箭。所以 toxic 的本义是\"弓箭的\"。",
    "Greek toxon (a bow) is a Scythian loan; toxikon (pharmakon) was the bow-drug — poison smeared on arrows. Rome borrowed toxicum for poison; toxicus poisonous (toxic 1660s), in-toxicare medieval Latin \"to poison\" — intoxicate meant drunkenness only from the 16th century, detoxify 1905, toxin 1886, toxicology 1799, antitoxin 1891, a toxophilite a lover of the bow (an archer), toxophily archery. Toxic began as \"of the bow\"."],
  ult: ["ultra(在那边)、ulterior(更远的)、ultimus(最远的、最后的)来自印欧语 *h₂el-(那边,与 alius 同根)加后缀。罗马人 ultra 是越过界线的;ultimus 最后的(ultimate 1650 年代,ultimatum 是外交上\"最后的话\"1730 年代),ulterior 更远——隐藏的动机(ulterior motive),ultra 前缀是 19 世纪的产物:ultra-violet 1840 年,ultra-sound 1911,ultra-marine 是\"海那边来的\"青金石蓝(1590 年代,从阿富汗进口),outrage 是 ultra-aticum\"过分\"经法语 outrage——与 rage 无关,utterance 与此无关。",
    "ultra (on the far side), ulterior (farther) and ultimus (farthest, last) come from PIE *h₂el- (beyond, kin to alius) with suffixes. Roman ultra is past the line; ultimus last (ultimate 1650s; an ultimatum is diplomacy's last word, 1730s), ulterior farther — a hidden purpose (ulterior motive), the prefix ultra is 19th-century: ultra-violet 1840, ultra-sound 1911, ultra-marine the lapis blue \"from beyond the sea\" (1590s, imported from Afghanistan), outrage is ultra-aticum, excess, through French outrage — nothing to do with rage."],
  celer: ["celer(快)来自印欧语 *kel-(驱赶、快),同根有希腊语 kelēs(快马)、kellein(驱赶)、梵语 kalayati(驱赶)。罗马早期的骑兵卫队叫 celeres(快的人);celeritas 速度(celerity),ac-celerare 加速(accelerate,伽利略用于落体),de-celerare 减速(1899),accelerator 1900 年代汽车油门,celerity 迅捷。",
    "celer (swift) comes from PIE *kel- (to drive, swift), shared with Greek kelēs (a racehorse), kellein (to drive), Sanskrit kalayati (drives). Rome's early royal bodyguard were the celeres, the swift ones; celeritas speed (celerity), ac-celerare to speed up (accelerate — Galileo's word for falling bodies), de-celerare to slow (1899), accelerator the car's pedal (1900s), celerity."],
  coron: ["corona(花冠、王冠)是希腊语 korōnē(弯曲物、乌鸦——弯喙)的借词,印欧语 *(s)ker-(弯)。罗马人的 corona 是花环、军功冠(corona civica 救公民一命的橡叶冠),后指王冠;coronare 加冕(coronation),corollarium 是\"小花冠\"——给演员的额外赏钱,后指推论(corollary 是定理\"附赠\"的结论),corolla 花冠,coroner 是国王的官员(custos placitorum coronae),coronary 是心脏上像王冠的血管(1670 年代),corona 也是日冕、病毒的冠状刺(coronavirus 1968),crown 经法语 corone,cornice 与此无关。",
    "corona (a garland, a crown) is a loan from Greek korōnē (a curved thing, a crow — for its hooked beak), PIE *(s)ker- (to bend). Roman corona was a wreath and a military decoration (the corona civica of oak leaves for saving a citizen's life), later a monarch's crown; coronare to crown (coronation), corollarium a little garland — the tip given to actors, later a deduction thrown in with a theorem (corollary), corolla the flower's crown, coroner the crown's officer (custos placitorum coronae), coronary the crown-like vessels of the heart (1670s), corona also the sun's halo and the virus's spikes (coronavirus 1968), crown through French corone."],
  pecc: ["peccare(犯错、跌倒)来自 pes(脚)——\"踩错\"、绊倒,与 pessimus(最坏的)同源。罗马人的 peccatum 是过失;基督教用它译希腊语 hamartia(射偏——罪),于是 peccatum 成了\"罪\"(péché、pecado、peccato),peccator 罪人,im-peccabilis 不会犯错的(impeccable),peccadillo 是西班牙语\"小罪\",peccant 犯错的,pessimus 是 ped-simus\"最跌倒的\"——pessimism 1794 年造(与 optimism 相对)。",
    "peccare (to stumble, to err) comes from pes (foot) — a misstep — and is kin to pessimus (worst). Roman peccatum was a fault; the Church used it to translate Greek hamartia (a missed shot — sin), so peccatum became sin (French péché, Spanish pecado, Italian peccato), peccator a sinner, im-peccabilis unable to sin (impeccable), peccadillo Spanish for a little sin, peccant erring, pessimus is ped-simus, \"most fallen\" — pessimism was coined in 1794 to match optimism."],
  tol: ["tollere(举起、拿走)与 tolerare(承受)来自印欧语 *telh₂-(举、承受),同根有希腊语 tlēnai(忍受)、Atlas(\"承受者\"——扛天的巨人)、talanton(天平、重量——talent 是称出的金银重量,后经《圣经》比喻转为才能)、英语 thole(忍受)。罗马人 tollere 举起孩子——父亲把新生儿抱起表示承认(tollere liberos);tolerare 承受(tolerate、tolerance 是 16 世纪宗教宽容的用语),ex-tollere 举高(extol),latus 是 ferre 的分词却出自 tollere(*tlātos),所以 translate、relate 也在这个根上,toll(通行费)与此无关。",
    "tollere (to lift, take away) and tolerare (to bear) come from PIE *telh₂- (to lift, endure), shared with Greek tlēnai (to endure), Atlas (the bearer — the giant holding up the sky), talanton (a balance, a weight — a talent was a weighed sum of silver, turned into ability by the Gospel parable), English thole (to endure). A Roman father tollere the newborn, lifting it to acknowledge it (tollere liberos); tolerare to bear (tolerate; tolerance was a 16th-century word for religious forbearance), ex-tollere to raise up (extol); latus, the participle of ferre, actually comes from tollere (*tlātos), so translate and relate sit on this root too; toll the fee is unrelated."],
  verg: ["vergere(倾向、转向)来自印欧语 *wer-(转)存疑,同根的可能有 vertere。罗马人 vergere 说山坡向某方倾斜;con-vergere 一起倾向(converge 1690 年代数学),di-vergere 分开倾向(diverge 1660 年代),verge 经法语 verge——先指权杖(virga 是另一个词,棒),后指边缘(on the verge of),vergence 眼科的辐辏(1900 年代)。",
    "vergere (to incline, turn) may come from PIE *wer- (to turn), with vertere as a possible cousin. Roman vergere said a slope leaned one way; con-vergere to incline together (converge, mathematical from the 1690s), di-vergere to incline apart (diverge 1660s), verge through French verge — first a rod (virga is a different word) then an edge (on the verge of), vergence the eyes' turning (1900s)."],
  algia: ["希腊语 algos(痛)词源不明,可能与 alegein(关心)有关。它是医学里最常用的\"痛\"后缀:neur-algia 神经痛(1807),my-algia 肌肉痛,arthr-algia 关节痛,ceph-algia 头痛,nost-algia 是 1688 年瑞士医生霍弗为译德语 Heimweh(乡愁)造的\"归家之痛\"——最初是被诊断的病,士兵会因此死亡,an-algesia 无痛(analgesic 止痛药 1870 年代),fibromy-algia 1976。",
    "Greek algos (pain) is of unknown origin, perhaps linked to alegein (to care for). It is medicine's commonest suffix of pain: neur-algia (1807), my-algia muscle pain, arthr-algia joint pain, ceph-algia headache, nost-algia coined in 1688 by the Swiss physician Hofer for German Heimweh — the pain of homecoming, at first a diagnosed disease that soldiers were said to die of, an-algesia absence of pain (analgesic 1870s), fibromy-algia 1976."],
  ectomy: ["希腊语 ek-tomē 是\"切出\"(ek 出 + temnein 切);temnein 来自印欧语 *tem-(切),同根有拉丁语 tondere(剪)、templum(切出的观测区)、tempus?、英语 无。外科在 19 世纪用 -ectomy 造词:tonsill-ectomy 1870 年代,append-ectomy 1890 年代,mast-ectomy 1920,hyster-ectomy 1886;-tomy 是切开(ana-tomy 切开研究、lobo-tomy 1936、dicho-tomy 一分为二),-stomy 是造口(colo-stomy),atom 是\"不可切\",epitome 切短的摘要,entomology 是\"切成节的\"——昆虫学。",
    "Greek ek-tomē is a cutting-out (ek out + temnein to cut); temnein comes from PIE *tem- (to cut), shared with Latin tondere (to shear) and templum (the sector cut out for augury). Surgery built its -ectomy words in the 19th century: tonsill-ectomy 1870s, append-ectomy 1890s, mast-ectomy 1920, hyster-ectomy 1886; -tomy is a cutting into (ana-tomy cutting up to study, lobo-tomy 1936, dicho-tomy cutting in two), -stomy a made opening (colo-stomy), atom the uncuttable, epitome a cut-down summary, entomology the study of the segmented — insects."],
  bib: ["bibere(喝)来自印欧语 *peh₃-(喝)的重叠式 *pi-ph₃-,同根有梵语 pibati、希腊语 pinō(pinein——symposium 是\"一起喝\")、英语 无(potion 是同根的 potare)。罗马人 bibere 喝;bibulus 爱喝的,im-bibere 吸入(imbibe),beverage 是 bibere 经法语 bevrage,bevy 与此无关?(可能),bibulous 好酒的,boire 法语、bere 意大利语、beber 西班牙语,bibber 酒徒,potio(饮料)给了 potion、poison(同一个词的法语形状!——drink 变 poison)。",
    "bibere (to drink) is PIE *peh₃- (to drink) in its reduplicated form *pi-ph₃-, shared with Sanskrit pibati and Greek pinō (a symposium is a drinking together); potare (to drink) is the same root. Romans bibere to drink; bibulus fond of drink, im-bibere to drink in (imbibe), beverage bibere through French bevrage, bibulous, French boire, Italian bere, Spanish beber, bibber a drinker, and potio (a drink) gave both potion and poison — one word in two French shapes, the drink that turned deadly."],
  coll: ["collum(脖子)来自印欧语 *kʷel-(转)——脖子是转动的部分,同根有英语 hals(古义脖子,德语 Hals)、wheel。罗马人的 collare 是狗或奴隶的项圈(collar 经法语),col 法语的脖子——衣领、山口(col),collier 项链,décolleté 露颈的,accolade 是 ad-collum\"搂脖子\"——中世纪册封骑士的拥抱,后指荣誉,colletage,collet 宝石托,torticollis 斜颈(1810 年代)。",
    "collum (neck) comes from PIE *kʷel- (to turn) — the neck is what turns, shared with German Hals (neck) and English wheel. A Roman collare was the collar of a dog or a slave (collar through French), French col the neck — a collar, a mountain pass, collier a necklace, décolleté bare-necked, accolade ad-collum, an embrace about the neck — the medieval dubbing of a knight, later any honour, collet a jewel's setting, torticollis a twisted neck (1810s)."],
  pisc: ["piscis(鱼)来自印欧语 *peisk-(鱼),同根有英语 fish、德语 Fisch、古爱尔兰语 īasc、哥特语 fisks——拉丁语 p 对应日耳曼语 f。罗马人 piscari 捕鱼,piscator 渔夫(piscatorial),piscina 鱼池——后指浴池、教堂的圣水池,Pisces 双鱼座,pesce 意大利语、poisson 法语、pez/pescado 西班牙语,porpoise 是 porcus piscis(猪鱼),piscivorous 食鱼的,Piscator 也是《钓客清话》里的渔夫。",
    "piscis (fish) comes from PIE *peisk- (fish), shared with English fish, German Fisch, Old Irish īasc, Gothic fisks — Latin p answers Germanic f. Romans piscari to fish, piscator a fisherman (piscatorial), piscina a fish-pond — later a bathing-pool and a church's font-basin, Pisces the zodiac's fishes, Italian pesce, French poisson, Spanish pez/pescado, porpoise porcus piscis, the pig-fish, piscivorous fish-eating, Piscator the angler in Walton's book."],
  vir: ["vir(男人)来自印欧语 *wiHrós(男人、英雄),同根有梵语 vīra(英雄)、古爱尔兰语 fer、英语 were-(werewolf 人狼)、world(wer-ald 人的时代)。罗马人的 virtus 就是\"男人该有的\"——勇武,后泛指美德(virtue),virilis 男子的(virile),vir 的敬语 vir clarissimus,tri-um-viri 三人执政(triumvirate),de-cem-viri 十人委员会,virago 女汉子,virtuoso 意大利语\"有德者\"——大师(1620 年代),virtual 是\"有内在效力的\"——虚拟义 20 世纪,werewolf 里的 were 是它的英语亲戚。",
    "vir (a man) comes from PIE *wiHrós (man, hero), shared with Sanskrit vīra (hero), Old Irish fer, English were- (werewolf, man-wolf) and world (wer-ald, the age of man). Roman virtus was what a man should have — valour, then virtue at large; virilis manly (virile), vir clarissimus the honorific, tri-um-viri three men in office (triumvirate), de-cem-viri the ten, virago a man-like woman, virtuoso Italian for a man of worth — a master (1620s), virtual having inherent force — the computer sense is 20th-century, and the were of werewolf is its English cousin."],
  vor: ["vorare(吞食)来自印欧语 *gʷerh₃-(吞),同根有希腊语 bibrōskein(吃)、bora(食物)、梵语 girati(吞)、英语 无(gorge 是同根的拉丁语 gurges 经法语)。罗马人 vorax 贪吃的(voracious),de-vorare 吞掉(devour),carni-vorus 食肉(carnivore),herbi-vorus 食草,omni-vorus 杂食(1650 年代),vora-city 贪婪,gurges 漩涡——gorge 咽喉、峡谷,regurgitate 回涌,gargle 与此无关(拟声)。",
    "vorare (to devour) comes from PIE *gʷerh₃- (to swallow), shared with Greek bibrōskein (to eat), bora (food), Sanskrit girati (swallows); gorge is the cognate Latin gurges through French. Roman vorax greedy (voracious), de-vorare to eat up (devour), carni-vorus flesh-eating (carnivore), herbi-vorus plant-eating, omni-vorus all-eating (1650s), voracity, gurges a whirlpool — gorge the throat, the ravine, regurgitate to throw back up."],
  pac: ["pax(和平,属格 pacis)来自 pangere(固定、订约),印欧语 *peh₂ǵ-(固定),同根有 pactum(契约)、pagus(界桩划出的乡区)、palus(桩)、希腊语 pēgnynai(固定)、英语 fang(抓牢)。罗马人的 pax 首先是\"订好的协议\"——Pax Romana 是靠武力固定的秩序;paci-ficus 造和平的(pacific;太平洋是麦哲伦 1520 年的 Mar Pacífico),pacificare 平定(pacify),pactum 条约(pact),pay 是 pacare\"使平息\"经法语 payer——付钱平息债主,appease 是 ad-pacem,peace 经法语 pais,pagan 是 paganus 乡下人(基督教用来称不信者),peasant 也是 pagus 的人。",
    "pax (peace, genitive pacis) comes from pangere (to fix, to settle), PIE *peh₂ǵ- (to fasten), shared with pactum (an agreement), pagus (a district staked out), palus (a stake), Greek pēgnynai (to fix), English fang (to seize). Roman pax was first a settled agreement — the Pax Romana was order fixed by force; paci-ficus peace-making (pacific; the Pacific was Magellan's Mar Pacífico of 1520), pacificare to pacify, pactum a pact, pay is pacare, to appease, through French payer — you pay to quiet the creditor, appease is ad-pacem, peace through French pais, pagan is paganus, a countryman (the Church's word for the unconverted), and peasant is also a man of the pagus."],
  bio: ["希腊语 bios(生命、生活方式)来自印欧语 *gʷeih₃-,与拉丁语 vivere、英语 quick 同根;希腊语另有 zōē(生物的生命)——bios 是\"一生\"、\"生涯\",所以 bio-graphia 是写一生(biography 1680 年代)。bio-logia 是拉马克与特雷维拉努斯 1802 年各自造的\"生命之学\",anti-biotic 1940 年代(\"对抗生命\"的药),bio-chemistry 1903,sym-biosis 共生 1877,micro-be 是 1878 年法语的\"小生命\",amphi-bios 两栖(两种生活),bio-psy 1879,bionic 1960,probiotic 1950 年代。",
    "Greek bios (life, a way of life) comes from PIE *gʷeih₃-, kin to Latin vivere and English quick; Greek also had zōē for animal life — bios was a lifetime, a career, so bio-graphia is the writing of a life (biography 1680s). bio-logia was coined independently by Lamarck and Treviranus in 1802, anti-biotic in the 1940s (a drug \"against life\"), bio-chemistry 1903, sym-biosis 1877, microbe 1878 French for a little life, amphi-bios living both ways (amphibian), bio-psy 1879, bionic 1960, probiotic 1950s."],
  crat: ["希腊语 kratos(力量、统治)来自印欧语 *kret-(硬、强),同根有英语 hard、哥特语 hardus。dēmo-kratia 是人民统治(democracy,雅典前 5 世纪),aristo-kratia 最优者统治,auto-kratōr 自己掌权,theo-kratia 是约瑟夫斯造的\"神治\",pluto-kratia 富人统治(1650 年代),bureau-cracy 是 1760 年代法国经济学家古尔奈的讥讽\"办公桌统治\",techno-cracy 1919,merito-cracy 是杨 1958 年的讽刺造词,-crat 后缀(democrat 1790 年代),Hippocrates 是\"马的力量\",Socrates\"完整的力量\"。",
    "Greek kratos (strength, rule) comes from PIE *kret- (hard, strong), shared with English hard and Gothic hardus. dēmo-kratia is rule by the people (democracy, 5th-century Athens), aristo-kratia rule by the best, auto-kratōr one who rules alone, theo-kratia Josephus' coinage for rule by God, pluto-kratia rule by the rich (1650s), bureau-cracy the French economist Gournay's 1760s jibe at \"desk-rule\", techno-cracy 1919, merito-cracy Michael Young's satirical coinage of 1958, the suffix -crat (democrat 1790s); Hippocrates is \"horse-power\", Socrates \"whole strength\"."],
  theo: ["希腊语 theos(神)词源不明——不与拉丁语 deus 同根(deus 是 *dyeu- 天;theos 可能来自 *dʰeh₁s-,神圣之事,与拉丁语 feriae 节日、fanum 神庙同根)。theo-logia 是柏拉图造的\"论神\"(theology),a-theos 无神,en-thousiasmos 是\"神在里面\"——附体的狂热(enthusiasm 17 世纪是贬义\"狂信\"),theo-kratia 神治,pan-theon 众神庙(万神殿),apo-theōsis 封神,theo-phania 神显,Timotheus 敬神者,Theodore 神的礼物,Dorothea 同义倒序,polytheism、monotheism 17 世纪,theodicy 是莱布尼茨 1710 年造的\"神义论\"。",
    "Greek theos (god) is of uncertain origin — not cognate with Latin deus (which is *dyeu-, sky); theos may come from *dʰeh₁s-, a sacred thing, kin to Latin feriae (festival) and fanum (shrine). theo-logia was Plato's word for discourse about the gods (theology), a-theos godless, en-thousiasmos having the god within — possession (enthusiasm was a 17th-century slur for fanaticism), theo-kratia rule by God, pan-theon the temple of all gods, apo-theōsis making into a god, theo-phania a divine appearance, Timotheus one who honours god, Theodore god's gift, Dorothea the same reversed, polytheism and monotheism 17th-century, theodicy Leibniz's 1710 justification of God."],
  anti: ["希腊语 anti(对面、代替)来自印欧语 *h₂enti(在前面、对着),同根有拉丁语 ante(在前)、梵语 anti、英语 and(古义\"对着\")、answer 里的 and-。希腊语的 anti 有两义:\"对抗\"(anti-thesis 对立、anti-dotos 对着给的药、Anti-christ 敌基督)和\"代替、相当\"(anti-pathēs 相反感受、antiphon 应答轮唱)。它成为英语最活跃的前缀之一:anti-body 1901,anti-biotic 1940 年代,anti-septic 1750 年代,anti-social 1797,antarctic 是\"对着北极\",antagonist 是\"对着争的人\"。注意拉丁语 ante-(在前:antecedent、antique)是它的亲戚但不是同一个前缀。",
    "Greek anti (opposite, instead of) comes from PIE *h₂enti (in front, facing), shared with Latin ante (before), Sanskrit anti, English and (originally \"facing\") and the and- of answer. Greek anti has two senses: against (anti-thesis, anti-dotos a drug given against, Anti-christ) and in place of, equivalent (anti-pathēs contrary feeling, antiphon the answering chant). It became one of English's busiest prefixes: anti-body 1901, anti-biotic 1940s, anti-septic 1750s, anti-social 1797, antarctic facing the Arctic, antagonist one who struggles against. Latin ante- (before: antecedent, antique) is its cousin, not the same prefix."],
  mono: ["希腊语 monos(单独、唯一)来自印欧语 *men-(小)存疑,同根的可能有拉丁语 minor?不,而是希腊语 manos(稀疏)。mon-archia 一人统治(monarchy),mon-achos 独居者——修士(monk 是 monachus 经古英语,monastery 独居处),mono-logos 独白,mono-gamia 一夫一妻,mono-theism 一神(1660 年代),mono-poly 独卖(monopoly 是亚里士多德的经济学词),mono-lithos 一块石(monolith),mono-tonos 单调,mono-graph 专著,mono-chrome 单色,monocle 单片镜(希腊拉丁混造),monad 是莱布尼茨的单子,monk 与 minster 同源。",
    "Greek monos (alone, single) is of uncertain origin; manos (sparse) may be a cousin. mon-archia rule by one (monarchy), mon-achos one who lives alone — a monk (monachus through Old English; a monastery is the solitary's dwelling), mono-logos a speech by one, mono-gamia one marriage, mono-theism one god (1660s), mono-poly a single seller (monopoly is Aristotle's economic term), mono-lithos a single stone (monolith), mono-tonos one tone (monotonous), mono-graph a treatise on one subject, mono-chrome one colour, monocle a Greek-Latin hybrid, monad Leibniz's unit; minster is monastery in Old English dress."],
  able: ["拉丁语后缀 -abilis / -ibilis 是 -bilis(\"能被…的\")加在动词词干上:ama-bilis 可爱的、credi-bilis 可信的、visi-bilis 可见的。它经法语 -able 进入英语后脱离了拉丁语,可以加在任何词上——包括日耳曼词(readable、drinkable、unthinkable),是英语最能产的后缀之一;-ible 保留在直接从拉丁语借的词里(possible、terrible、legible)。able 单独作形容词是 habilis(好拿的、能干的)经法语 hable——与后缀无关但长得一样,ability 是 habilitas。capable 是晚期拉丁语 capabilis(能容纳的)。",
    "The Latin suffix -abilis / -ibilis is -bilis (\"that can be …ed\") on a verb stem: ama-bilis lovable, credi-bilis believable, visi-bilis visible. Entering English through French as -able it cut loose from Latin and can attach to anything — Germanic words included (readable, drinkable, unthinkable), making it one of English's most productive suffixes; -ible survives in words borrowed straight from Latin (possible, terrible, legible). The adjective able by itself is habilis (handy, capable) through French hable — unrelated to the suffix though identical in shape, and ability is habilitas. capable is late Latin capabilis, able to hold."],
  dign: ["dignus(配得上的)来自印欧语 *deḱ-(接受、合适),与 decet(合适)、docere(教)、希腊语 dokein 同根。罗马人的 dignitas 是与身份相称的尊严——西塞罗把 dignitas 与 otium(安宁)并列为政治理想;dignari 认为配得上(deign 经法语 deigner——屈尊),in-dignus 不配的(indignant 是为\"不配\"而愤慨),dignitary 显贵,dignify 使尊贵,con-dignus 相称(condign punishment),dainty 竟是 dignitas 经法语 deintié——\"珍贵的\"——精致的,disdain 是 dis-dignari 觉得不配。",
    "dignus (worthy) comes from PIE *deḱ- (to take, be fitting), kin to decet (it suits), docere (to teach), Greek dokein. Roman dignitas was the standing that befits one's rank — Cicero paired dignitas with otium as the goals of politics; dignari to deem worthy (deign through French deigner — to condescend), in-dignus unworthy (indignant is outrage at the unworthy), a dignitary a person of rank, dignify, con-dignus fitting (condign punishment), dainty is dignitas through French deintié — a precious thing — hence delicate, disdain is dis-dignari, to think unworthy."],
  err: ["errare(游荡、犯错)来自印欧语 *h₁ers-(动、游荡),同根有哥特语 airzjan(误导)、德语 irren(迷路、犯错)、Irre(疯人)、英语 无直接(race 是古挪威语 rás 奔跑?不同)。罗马人 errare 先是漫游(errans stella 漫游的星——行星),后指出错——Errare humanum est(犯错是人之常情,塞涅卡);error 错误,erraticus 漫游的(erratic 不稳定),aberrare 走偏(aberration),knight-errant 是游侠(errant 经法语 errer 漫游——也混入了 iter 旅行),erroneous 错误的,erratum 勘误。",
    "errare (to wander, to err) comes from PIE *h₁ers- (to move, wander), shared with Gothic airzjan (to lead astray), German irren (to lose one's way, to err), Irre (madness). Roman errare was first to roam (errans stella, a wandering star — a planet), then to go wrong — Errare humanum est, to err is human (Seneca); error, erraticus wandering (erratic), aberrare to stray (aberration), a knight-errant roams (errant through French errer, mixed with iter, a journey), erroneous, erratum a slip to be corrected."],
  fract: ["frangere(打破)的分词 fractus 是这一族的主干(见 frango):fractio 打破——分数是\"打碎的数\"(fraction,中世纪拉丁语),fractura 骨折(fracture),frag-mentum 碎片(fragment),fragilis 易碎(fragile;frail 是它经法语 fraile),re-fractio 光线折断(refraction,开普勒),in-fractio 违反(infraction),re-fractarius 抗拒的(refractory),suf-fragium 是\"碎瓦片\"——投票用的陶片(suffrage 选举权),saxi-fraga 碎石草(虎耳草),naufragium 沉船,diffraction 是格里马尔迪 1660 年代的\"光的破散\",fractal 是芒德布罗 1975 年的\"碎形\"。",
    "The participle fractus of frangere (to break) carries this family (see frango): fractio a breaking — a fraction is a broken number (medieval Latin), fractura a fracture, frag-mentum a fragment, fragilis breakable (fragile; frail is its French form fraile), re-fractio the bending-back of light (refraction, Kepler), in-fractio a breaking of rules (infraction), re-fractarius resisting (refractory), suf-fragium a potsherd — the ballot token (suffrage), saxi-fraga the rock-breaker plant, naufragium shipwreck, diffraction Grimaldi's 1660s \"breaking apart\" of light, fractal Mandelbrot's 1975 broken shape."],
  greg: ["grex(畜群,属格 gregis)来自印欧语 *h₂ger-(聚集),同根有希腊语 ageirein(聚集)、agora(市集)、梵语 grāma(村——聚落)。罗马人 grex 是羊群、也是戏班;gregarius 群居的——普通士兵是 miles gregarius(gregarious 合群的),con-gregare 聚成群(congregation 会众),se-gregare 从群中分出(segregate;美国种族隔离义 1900 年代),ag-gregare 归群(aggregate 总计),e-gregius 是\"从群里挑出的\"——出众(egregious 到 16 世纪才转贬义),Gregory 与此无关(希腊语 grēgorein 警醒)。",
    "grex (a flock, genitive gregis) comes from PIE *h₂ger- (to gather), shared with Greek ageirein (to assemble), agora (the market), Sanskrit grāma (a village — a gathering). Roman grex was a flock and a troupe of actors; gregarius of the herd — a common soldier was a miles gregarius (gregarious, sociable), con-gregare to flock together (congregation), se-gregare to set apart from the flock (segregate; the racial sense is 1900s American), ag-gregare to add to the flock (aggregate), e-gregius picked out of the flock — outstanding (egregious turned bad only in the 16th century); Gregory is unrelated (Greek grēgorein, to be watchful)."],
  migr: ["migrare(迁移)来自印欧语 *mei-(换、去),同根有希腊语 ameibein(交换——amoeba 是\"变形的\")、梵语 mināti。罗马人 migrare 搬家;e-migrare 移出(emigrate 1770 年代),im-migrare 移入(immigrate 1620 年代,美洲用语),trans-migrare 迁徙——灵魂转世(transmigration),migratio 迁徙(migration 候鸟义 1640 年代),migrant 1670 年代,migraine 与此无关(hemicrania 半头痛)。",
    "migrare (to move house) comes from PIE *mei- (to change, go), shared with Greek ameibein (to exchange — an amoeba is the changing one), Sanskrit mināti. Romans migrare to move; e-migrare to move out (emigrate 1770s), im-migrare to move in (immigrate 1620s, an American usage), trans-migrare to pass across — the soul's transmigration, migratio migration (of birds from the 1640s), migrant 1670s; migraine is unrelated (hemicrania, half-head pain)."],
  glob: ["globus(球、团)可能与 glomus(线团)同源,印欧语 *gel-(聚成团),同根有英语 clew/clue(线团——忒修斯的线)、cling、club。罗马人 globus 是球体、也是人群;globulus 小球(globule),globosus 球形,con-globare 团成球,globe 经法语 globe(地球仪 1550 年代),global 1670 年代指球形的,\"全球的\"义 1890 年代,globalization 1959,glomerulus 肾小球,conglomerate 卷成一团——企业集团(1960 年代),hemoglobin 血红蛋白(\"血球\")。",
    "globus (a ball, a mass) is probably kin to glomus (a ball of thread), PIE *gel- (to form into a ball), shared with English clew/clue (a ball of thread — Theseus'), cling, club. Roman globus was a sphere and a crowd; globulus a little ball (globule), globosus round, con-globare to roll into a ball, globe through French globe (a terrestrial globe 1550s), global meant spherical in the 1670s, worldwide from the 1890s, globalization 1959, glomerulus the kidney's little ball, conglomerate rolled together — a business group (1960s), haemoglobin the blood-globule protein."],
  prud: ["prudens 是 providens(有远见的)的缩合——pro-videre 提前看。罗马法学家自称 prudentes(有见识的人),iuris-prudentia 就是他们的学问;prudentia 是四枢德之首——实践智慧(prudence),im-prudens 无远见的(imprudent),prudery 是法语 prude(正经女人,从 prudefemme 贤淑妇人来)的派生——与 prudens 同源,jurisprudence 法学,prudential 谨慎的,providence 是同一个词未缩合的形式——天意。",
    "prudens is providens (foreseeing) contracted — pro-videre, to see ahead. Rome's jurists called themselves prudentes, men of insight, and iuris-prudentia was their science; prudentia headed the four cardinal virtues — practical wisdom (prudence), im-prudens unforeseeing (imprudent), prudery derives from French prude (a proper woman, from prudefemme, a worthy woman) — the same root, jurisprudence, prudential, and providence is the same word uncontracted — foresight, God's care."],
  tim: ["timere(害怕)是拉丁语独有的词,词源不明。罗马人 timor 恐惧,timidus 胆小的(timid),in-timidare 使害怕(intimidate 1640 年代),timere Deum 敬畏神(timor Domini),Timothy 与此无关(希腊语 timē 荣誉),timorous 胆怯的(经法语),temerity 与此无关(temere 盲目地),Timon 无关。",
    "timere (to fear) is a purely Latin word of unknown origin. Roman timor is fear, timidus fearful (timid), in-timidare to frighten (intimidate 1640s), timere Deum to fear God (timor Domini), timorous through French; Timothy is unrelated (Greek timē, honour), and so is temerity (temere, rashly)."],
  icon: ["希腊语 eikōn(像、肖像)来自 eoika(像、相似),印欧语 *weik-(像)存疑。拜占庭的 eikōn 是圣像;8 世纪的 eikono-klastēs(破坏圣像者)引发了圣像破坏运动(iconoclasm;iconoclast 16 世纪用于新教徒),eikono-graphia 图像志(iconography 17 世纪),icon 在 1982 年成了电脑屏幕上的小图标,iconic 1650 年代指肖像的,20 世纪转为\"标志性的\",iconostasis 圣像屏。",
    "Greek eikōn (an image, a likeness) comes from eoika (to be like), perhaps PIE *weik- (to resemble). The Byzantine eikōn was a holy image; the 8th-century eikono-klastēs, image-breakers, made iconoclasm (iconoclast was applied to Protestants in the 16th century), eikono-graphia the description of images (iconography 17th century), icon became a small computer picture in 1982, iconic meant of a portrait in the 1650s and emblematic in the 20th century, iconostasis the icon-screen."],
  pract: ["希腊语 prassein(做、办事)的名词 praxis 是\"做\"——与 theōria(看)相对;prāktikos 是能办事的——practical 经拉丁语 practicus(1400 年代),practice 中世纪拉丁语 practica(医生、律师的执业),practitioner 从业者,pragma 是\"做成的事\"——事务,prāgmatikos 务实的(pragmatic;pragmatism 是皮尔士 1878 年的哲学名),chiro-practic 是 1898 年造的\"手做\",malpractice 1670 年代。",
    "The noun praxis of Greek prassein (to do, to manage) is doing — set against theōria, seeing; prāktikos is fit for action — practical through Latin practicus (1400s), practice medieval Latin practica (the working of a doctor or lawyer), practitioner, pragma a thing done — an affair, prāgmatikos businesslike (pragmatic; pragmatism was Peirce's philosophical name of 1878), chiro-practic coined in 1898 as \"done by hand\", malpractice 1670s."],
  rhythm: ["希腊语 rhythmos(节奏、比例)来自 rhein(流),印欧语 *sreu-(流),同根有梵语 sravati、英语 stream、德语 Strom、俄语 struya。希腊人的 rhythmos 是流动的规律——诗和舞的节奏(柏拉图定义为\"运动的秩序\"),罗马人借为 rhythmus;rhythmic 1600 年代,rhyme 是同一个词经法语 rime 的变形(拼法 rhyme 是 17 世纪仿 rhythm 改的),rheum 是\"流出的\"体液(rheumatism 是体液流到关节),cat-arrh 向下流(鼻炎),dia-rrhoea 穿过流,logo-rrhoea 话如流水,haemo-rrhage 血流,rheo-stat 1843,arrhythmia 心律不齐。",
    "Greek rhythmos (measured motion, proportion) comes from rhein (to flow), PIE *sreu- (to flow), shared with Sanskrit sravati, English stream, German Strom, Russian struya. Greek rhythmos was the order in flowing movement — the beat of verse and dance (Plato defined it as order in motion), borrowed by Rome as rhythmus; rhythmic 1600s, rhyme is the same word through French rime (the spelling rhyme was remodelled on rhythm in the 17th century), rheum the humour that flows out (rheumatism, humour flowing to the joints), cat-arrh a flowing down, dia-rrhoea a flowing through, logo-rrhoea a flood of words, haemo-rrhage a bursting of blood, rheo-stat 1843, arrhythmia."],
  strat: ["拉丁语有两个 strat-。stratum 是 sternere(铺)的分词——铺开的层:strata 地层(1590 年代地质学),stratify 分层,strategy 与此无关;street 是 via strata 铺过的路(古英语从拉丁借),estrade 台,prostrate 铺倒在地,consternation 是\"被打倒在地\"。希腊语 stratos(军队)来自 *ster-(铺开)——军队是铺开在平原上的:stratēgos 将军(stratos 军队 + agein 引导),stratēgia 将军的技艺(strategy 1810 年代),stratagem 计谋(1490 年代),stratocracy 军人统治。两个 strat- 其实同出一个\"铺开\"。",
    "Latin has two strat-. stratum is the participle of sternere (to spread) — a spread layer: strata (geology, 1590s), stratify, street is via strata, a paved road (borrowed into Old English from Latin), estrade a platform, prostrate spread flat, consternation being thrown to the ground. Greek stratos (an army) comes from *ster- (to spread) — an army spread over the plain: stratēgos a general (stratos + agein, to lead), stratēgia the general's art (strategy 1810s), stratagem a trick of war (1490s), stratocracy rule by the army. The two strat- share one act of spreading."],
  ur: ["希腊语 ouron(尿)来自印欧语 *h₁worseh₂-(尿、雨),同根有拉丁语 urina、梵语 vārṣati(下雨)、英语 无。罗马人 urina 尿(urine),urinare 竟是\"潜水\"(urinator 潜水员!),uro-logia 1840 年代,urea 尿素(1806),uraemia 尿毒症,di-uretic 利尿(经 dia-ourein 穿过排尿),en-uresis 遗尿,urethra 尿道,uric acid 1799。",
    "Greek ouron (urine) comes from PIE *h₁worseh₂- (urine, rain), shared with Latin urina and Sanskrit vārṣati (it rains). Roman urina is urine, but urinare meant to dive (a urinator was a diver!), uro-logia 1840s, urea (1806), uraemia, di-uretic (via dia-ourein, to pass urine through), en-uresis bed-wetting, urethra, uric acid 1799."],
  dens: ["densus(密、稠)来自印欧语 *dens-(密)存疑,同根的可能有希腊语 dasys(浓密的)。罗马人 densus 是树林茂密、雾浓;densitas 密度(density 1600 年代物理义),con-densare 压密——凝结(condense、condensation),densimeter,dense 1590 年代指稠密,\"迟钝\"义 1820 年代。",
    "densus (thick, close) may come from PIE *dens- (thick), with Greek dasys (bushy) as a possible cousin. Roman densus was a thick wood, a heavy fog; densitas density (the physical sense 1600s), con-densare to make dense — to condense (condensation), densimeter, dense meant thick in the 1590s and slow-witted from the 1820s."],
  digit: ["digitus(手指、脚趾)来自印欧语 *deiḱ-(指出),与 dicere 同根——手指是指东西的。罗马人用手指计数,所以 digitus 也是\"一指宽\"(约 1.85 厘米);digitalis 手指的(毛地黄 Digitalis 是 1542 年富克斯从德语 Fingerhut\"指套\"译的),digit 从手指到数字(15 世纪——十个手指十个数字),digital 1650 年代指手指的,1940 年代指数字计算的,prestidigitation 快手戏法(法语 preste 快),digitize 1953。",
    "digitus (finger, toe) comes from PIE *deiḱ- (to point out), kin to dicere — the finger points. Romans counted on their fingers, so digitus was also a finger's breadth (about 1.85 cm); digitalis of the finger (the foxglove Digitalis was Fuchs' 1542 rendering of German Fingerhut, thimble), digit went from finger to numeral in the 15th century — ten fingers, ten figures, digital meant of the fingers in the 1650s and of computers from the 1940s, prestidigitation nimble-finger conjuring (French preste, quick), digitize 1953."],
  emin: ["minere(突出)只见于复合词,来自印欧语 *men-(突出),同根有 mons(山)、mentum(下巴)、威尔士语 mynydd(山)、英语 mouth?否。e-minere 突出来(eminent 显赫,eminence 是枢机主教的尊称,1650 年代),im-minere 悬在上面(imminent 迫近),pro-minere 向前突出(prominent),minae 是突出的墙头——威胁(menace 经法语,minatory),promontory 突出的岬角,mons 山(mountain、amount 是 ad montem 上山——数量,paramount 是 par amont 在上面),minatory 威胁的。",
    "minere (to jut out) survives only in compounds, from PIE *men- (to project), shared with mons (mountain), mentum (chin), Welsh mynydd (mountain). e-minere to stand out (eminent; Eminence the cardinal's title from the 1650s), im-minere to overhang (imminent), pro-minere to jut forward (prominent), minae the jutting battlements — threats (menace through French, minatory), promontory a jutting headland, mons a mountain (mountain, amount is ad montem, uphill — a sum, paramount par amont, above), minatory threatening."],
  memb: ["membrum(肢体、成员)可能来自印欧语 *mēms-(肉),同根有希腊语 mēros(大腿)、梵语 māṃsa(肉)、哥特语 mimz(肉)。罗马人的 membra 是四肢,也是团体的成员——身体的隐喻(member 经法语 membre),membrana 是包肢体的皮——羊皮纸、薄膜(membrane),dis-membrare 肢解(dismember),re-memorari 与此无关(memor),membership 1640 年代。",
    "membrum (a limb, a member) may come from PIE *mēms- (flesh), shared with Greek mēros (thigh), Sanskrit māṃsa (meat), Gothic mimz (flesh). Roman membra were the limbs, and the members of a body politic — the bodily metaphor (member through French membre), membrana the skin over a limb — parchment, a thin film (membrane), dis-membrare to tear limb from limb (dismember), membership 1640s."],
  orb: ["orbis(圆、圆盘、世界)是拉丁语独有的词,词源不明。罗马人的 orbis terrarum 是\"大地之圆\"——世界(urbi et orbi 向城与世界);orbita 是车轮碾出的圆道——轨迹(orbit 天文义是开普勒时代,1690 年代;眼眶 orbit 是眼球的圆窝),orbicularis 圆的(orbicular),ex-orbitans 出轨的——过分(exorbitant),orb 球体,suborbital 亚轨道,orbis 也是罗马的阅兵圆阵。",
    "orbis (a circle, a disc, the world) is a purely Latin word of unknown origin. The Roman orbis terrarum was the circle of lands — the world (urbi et orbi, to the city and the world); orbita the round track a wheel wears — a path (the astronomical orbit dates from Kepler's age, 1690s; the eye's orbit is its round socket), orbicularis circular (orbicular), ex-orbitans off the track — excessive (exorbitant), orb a sphere, suborbital; orbis was also a Roman circular battle formation."],
  sorb: ["sorbere(吸、啜)来自印欧语 *srebʰ-(啜饮),同根有希腊语 rhophein(啜)、立陶宛语 srebti、亚美尼亚语 arbi(我喝)。罗马人 sorbere 啜汤;ab-sorbere 吸走(absorb 1590 年代),ad-sorbere 吸附(1882 年物理化学新造),re-sorbere 再吸收(resorb),sorbet 与此无关(阿拉伯语 sharba 饮料),absorbent 吸水的,absorption 1590 年代。",
    "sorbere (to suck in, sip) comes from PIE *srebʰ- (to sip), shared with Greek rhophein (to gulp), Lithuanian srebti, Armenian arbi (I drank). Romans sorbere soup; ab-sorbere to suck away (absorb 1590s), ad-sorbere to hold on a surface (adsorb, a physical chemist's coinage of 1882), re-sorbere to take back in (resorb), absorbent, absorption 1590s; sorbet is unrelated (Arabic sharba, a drink)."],
  tenu: ["tenuis(薄、细)来自印欧语 *ten-(拉伸)——拉薄的,同根有 tendere、希腊语 tanaos(伸长的)、英语 thin、德语 dünn、梵语 tanu(细)。罗马人 tenuis 是细线、稀薄的空气、微弱的希望;tenuitas 细薄,at-tenuare 使变薄(attenuate;疫苗的减毒),ex-tenuare 使变薄——减轻罪责(extenuating circumstances),tenuous 稀薄的——站不住脚的(1590 年代),thin 是它的日耳曼亲戚。",
    "tenuis (thin, fine) comes from PIE *ten- (to stretch) — stretched thin, shared with tendere, Greek tanaos (outstretched), English thin, German dünn, Sanskrit tanu (slender). Roman tenuis was a fine thread, thin air, a slender hope; tenuitas thinness, at-tenuare to make thin (attenuate — of a weakened vaccine), ex-tenuare to thin out — to lessen guilt (extenuating circumstances), tenuous thin — flimsy (1590s); thin is its Germanic cousin."],
  vap: ["vapor(蒸汽)可能来自印欧语 *kʷep-(冒烟、沸腾),同根有希腊语 kapnos(烟)、立陶宛语 kvapas(气味)。罗马人 vapor 是热气、蒸汽;vaporarium 浴场的蒸汽室,e-vaporare 蒸发(evaporate 1540 年代),vaporosus 多雾的,vapours 是 17-18 世纪贵妇的\"忧郁症\"(体液蒸汽上冲脑),vaporizer 1870 年代,vapid 是 vapidus——酒跑了气的——乏味,vaporetto 威尼斯的汽船。",
    "vapor (steam) may come from PIE *kʷep- (to smoke, boil), shared with Greek kapnos (smoke) and Lithuanian kvapas (a smell). Roman vapor was warm exhalation, steam; a vaporarium the baths' steam room, e-vaporare to turn to vapour (evaporate 1540s), vaporosus misty, the vapours the fashionable melancholy of 17th–18th-century ladies (humoral fumes rising to the brain), vaporizer 1870s, vapid is vapidus — wine gone flat — insipid, the vaporetto Venice's steam-boat."],
  troph: ["希腊语 trephein(养育、使浓稠)的名词 trophē 是营养;印欧语 *dʰrebʰ-(凝结)。a-trophia 无营养——萎缩(atrophy 1610 年代),hyper-trophia 过度营养——肥大(1834),dys-trophia 营养不良(1880 年代),auto-troph 自养生物(1938),trophic 营养的,trophy 是另一个词:tropaion 是敌人\"转身\"逃跑处立的纪念(tropē 转向)——与 trope 比喻同根。",
    "The noun trophē of Greek trephein (to nourish, to thicken) is nourishment; PIE *dʰrebʰ- (to curdle). a-trophia lack of nourishment — wasting (atrophy 1610s), hyper-trophia over-nourishment — enlargement (1834), dys-trophia bad nourishment (1880s), auto-troph self-feeding organism (1938), trophic. Trophy is a different word: a tropaion was the monument set where the enemy \"turned\" to flee (tropē, a turning) — kin to trope, a figure of speech."],
  zoo: ["希腊语 zōon(动物)来自 zōē(生命),印欧语 *gʷeih₃-(活),与拉丁语 vivere 同根。zōi-dion 是\"小动物\"——黄道带 zōdiakos(kyklos)是\"动物的圈\"(zodiac),zōo-logia 1660 年代,zoological garden 1829 年伦敦摄政公园——zoo 是它的缩写(1847),proto-zoa 最初的动物(1830 年代),zōo-phytos 植虫,Zoe 名字是\"生命\",azo- 化学的\"无生命\"(氮),zoophobia,Mesozoic 中生代(\"中间动物\"),Palaeozoic 古生代。",
    "Greek zōon (an animal) comes from zōē (life), PIE *gʷeih₃- (to live), kin to Latin vivere. A zōi-dion is a little animal — the zōdiakos (kyklos) is the circle of animals (zodiac), zōo-logia 1660s, the Zoological Gardens opened in Regent's Park in 1829 and zoo is their clipping (1847), proto-zoa the first animals (1830s), zōo-phytos a plant-animal, Zoe the name means life, azo- chemistry's \"lifeless\" (nitrogen), zoophobia, Mesozoic the middle-animal era, Palaeozoic the ancient-animal one."],
  magnet: ["希腊语 Magnēs lithos 是\"马格尼西亚的石头\"——小亚细亚马格尼西亚出产的天然磁石(也说是色萨利的马格尼西亚);泰勒斯已知它吸铁。罗马人借为 magnes;magneticus 1610 年代(吉尔伯特 1600 年的《论磁》奠定磁学),magnetism 1610 年代,electro-magnetic 1821(法拉第时代),magnesia(氧化镁)与 manganese(锰)都来自同一个地名——两种矿石都叫\"马格尼西亚石\",magneto 1880 年代,animal magnetism 是梅斯梅尔的催眠术。",
    "Greek Magnēs lithos is the stone of Magnesia — the natural lodestone from Magnesia in Asia Minor (or the one in Thessaly); Thales already knew it drew iron. Rome borrowed it as magnes; magneticus 1610s (Gilbert's De Magnete of 1600 founded the science), magnetism 1610s, electro-magnetic 1821 (Faraday's age), magnesia and manganese both come from the same place-name — two ores called Magnesian stone, magneto 1880s, animal magnetism Mesmer's hypnotism."],
  harmon: ["希腊语 harmonia(接合、协调)来自 harmos(关节、接缝),印欧语 *h₂er-(装配),与拉丁语 ars、arma、英语 arm 同根。木匠的 harmonia 是榫接;毕达哥拉斯派把它用于音程——\"音的接合\",Harmonia 也是战神与爱神的女儿;harmonicus 音乐的,harmony 经法语,harmonica 是富兰克林 1762 年给玻璃琴起的名,后转给口琴,phil-harmonic 爱乐的(1813 年伦敦爱乐协会),harmonize 1480 年代,harmonium 1840 年代。",
    "Greek harmonia (a joining, a fitting together) comes from harmos (a joint, a seam), PIE *h₂er- (to fit), kin to Latin ars and arma and English arm. A carpenter's harmonia was a mortise joint; the Pythagoreans applied it to musical intervals — the fitting of tones, and Harmonia was the daughter of Ares and Aphrodite; harmonicus musical, harmony through French, harmonica Franklin's 1762 name for his glass instrument, later the mouth-organ, phil-harmonic music-loving (London's Philharmonic Society, 1813), harmonize 1480s, harmonium 1840s."],
  meteor: ["希腊语 meteōros(高高在上的)是 meta(上)+ aeirein(举起);ta meteōra 是\"天上的事\"——亚里士多德的《气象学》(Meteorologica)讨论一切大气现象:雨、风、彗星、流星。所以 meteorology 至今是气象学而非陨石学(1610 年代);meteor 在 16 世纪泛指任何大气现象,19 世纪才专指流星,meteorite 陨石 1824,meteoric 一闪而过的(1830 年代),meteoroid 1865。",
    "Greek meteōros (raised high) is meta (above) + aeirein (to lift); ta meteōra were things aloft — Aristotle's Meteorologica treated every atmospheric phenomenon: rain, wind, comets, shooting stars. That is why meteorology is still the science of weather, not of meteorites (1610s); meteor meant any atmospheric event in the 16th century and narrowed to shooting stars in the 19th, meteorite 1824, meteoric brief and brilliant (1830s), meteoroid 1865."],
  ed: ["edere(吃)来自印欧语 *h₁ed-(吃),同根有希腊语 edein、英语 eat、德语 essen、梵语 admi、俄语 est'——拉丁语 est(他吃)与 est(他是)同形。罗马人 edere 吃;edulis 可食的(edible 1590 年代),es-culentus 可吃的(esculent),ob-esus 是\"吃得多的\"——肥胖(obese 1650 年代),com-edere 吃光(comestible 食物,法语 comestibles),edax 贪吃的(tempus edax rerum——吞噬万物的时间,奥维德),prandium 午餐是另一个词,manducare(嚼)给了法语 manger 和意大利语 mangiare——法语和意大利语的\"吃\"不是 edere。",
    "edere (to eat) comes from PIE *h₁ed- (to eat), shared with Greek edein, English eat, German essen, Sanskrit admi, Russian est' — Latin est (he eats) looks exactly like est (he is). Romans edere to eat; edulis eatable (edible 1590s), es-culentus fit to eat (esculent), ob-esus \"having eaten much\" — fat (obese 1650s), com-edere to eat up (comestible; French comestibles, food), edax devouring (tempus edax rerum — time that devours all things, Ovid); manducare (to chew) gave French manger and Italian mangiare — their words for eating are not edere."],
  s: ["意大利语和西班牙语的前缀 s- / es- 是拉丁语 ex-(出)或 dis-(分开、相反)磨损后的形式:意大利语 s-coprire 揭开(dis-cooperire)、s-fortuna 不幸(dis-)、s-fuggire 逃脱(ex-)、s-piegare 摊开、解释(ex-plicare)、s-vegliare 唤醒(ex-vigilare);西班牙语 es-coger 挑选(ex-colligere)、es-conder 藏(abs-condere)、es-capar 逃(ex-cappa 脱斗篷)。因为 ex- 和 dis- 在口语里都缩成一个 s,意大利语的 s- 既能表示\"出\"也能表示\"反\"(s-comodo 不便、s-leale 不忠)。",
    "The Italian and Spanish prefix s- / es- is Latin ex- (out) or dis- (apart, opposite) worn down: Italian s-coprire to uncover (dis-cooperire), s-fortuna misfortune (dis-), s-fuggire to escape (ex-), s-piegare to unfold, explain (ex-plicare), s-vegliare to wake (ex-vigilare); Spanish es-coger to choose (ex-colligere), es-conder to hide (abs-condere), es-capar to escape (ex-cappa, out of the cloak). Because ex- and dis- both shrank to a single s in speech, Italian s- can mean either out or un- (s-comodo uncomfortable, s-leale disloyal)."],
  omni: ["omnis(全部)是拉丁语独有的词,词源不明——可能来自 *op-ni-(丰富的,与 ops 同根)。罗马人 omnes 是所有人;omni-potens 全能(omnipotent,基督教对上帝的称呼),omni-sciens 全知,omni-praesens 无所不在,omni-vorus 杂食,omni-bus 是 1820 年代巴黎公共马车的名字——\"给所有人的车\"(与格复数),bus 是它的缩写(1832),omnium gatherum 大杂烩(戏仿拉丁语),Omnia vincit amor(爱征服一切,维吉尔)。",
    "omnis (all) is a purely Latin word of uncertain origin — perhaps *op-ni- (abundant, kin to ops). Roman omnes are everyone; omni-potens all-powerful (omnipotent, the Church's title for God), omni-sciens all-knowing, omni-praesens everywhere present, omni-vorus all-eating, omni-bus the name of the 1820s Paris public coach — \"for all\" (the dative plural), clipped to bus in 1832, omnium gatherum mock-Latin for a hotchpotch, Omnia vincit amor — love conquers all (Virgil)."],
  tele: ["希腊语 tēle(远)来自印欧语 *kʷel-(远)存疑,同根的可能有 telos(终点)、palai(很久以前)。它几乎全是现代造词:tele-scope 是 1611 年为伽利略的仪器起的名(\"远看\"),tele-graph 1790 年代查普的信号塔,tele-phone 1830 年代先指传声装置、1876 贝尔,tele-pathy 1882,tele-vision 1900 年巴黎博览会上的构想词,tele-gram 1852,tele-metry 1885,tele-commuting 1974,teleology 是 telos(目的)的——另一个词。",
    "Greek tēle (far) may come from PIE *kʷel- (far), with telos (an end) and palai (long ago) as possible cousins. Its family is almost all modern coinage: tele-scope was named in 1611 for Galileo's instrument (far-seeing), tele-graph in the 1790s for Chappe's signal towers, tele-phone for speaking-tubes in the 1830s and for Bell's device in 1876, tele-pathy 1882, tele-vision a concept word from the Paris exposition of 1900, tele-gram 1852, tele-metry 1885, tele-commuting 1974; teleology belongs to telos (purpose), a different word."],
  anim: ["animus(心灵、勇气)与 anima(气息、灵魂)来自印欧语 *h₂enh₁-(呼吸),同根有希腊语 anemos(风)、梵语 aniti(呼吸)、古爱尔兰语 anal(呼吸)、哥特语 us-anan(呼气)。罗马人区分 anima(生命之气,人与动物都有)和 animus(思想、意志、脾气);animal 是\"有气息的\"(animal),animare 赋予生命(animate;动画 animation 1912),un-animis 一心(unanimous),magn-animus 大心,pusill-animis 小心(胆怯),equ-animitas 心平,animositas 先是勇气、后指敌意(animosity 15 世纪转义),animadvert 把心转向——批评,anemone 是希腊语\"风之花\"。",
    "animus (mind, spirit) and anima (breath, soul) come from PIE *h₂enh₁- (to breathe), shared with Greek anemos (wind), Sanskrit aniti (breathes), Old Irish anal (breath), Gothic us-anan (to breathe out). Romans distinguished anima, the breath of life that beasts share, from animus, thought, will, temper; animal is the breathing creature, animare to give life (animate; animation as film 1912), un-animis of one mind (unanimous), magn-animus great-souled, pusill-animis small-souled (faint-hearted), equ-animitas evenness of mind, animositas courage first, then hostility (animosity shifted in the 15th century), animadvert to turn the mind toward — to criticise, anemone the Greek wind-flower."],
  dox: ["希腊语 doxa(意见、名声)来自 dokein(看来、以为),印欧语 *deḱ-(接受),与拉丁语 decet、dignus、docere 同根。柏拉图把 doxa(意见)与 epistēmē(知识)对立;ortho-doxos 正见(orthodox 是 4 世纪教会用语),hetero-doxos 异见,para-doxos 违反常见(paradox),doxo-logia 是\"荣耀之言\"(doxology 荣耀颂——doxa 在《圣经》里译希伯来语 kavod\"荣耀\"),dogma 是\"看来对的\"——决议、教义(dogmatic 1600 年代),Eudoxus 是\"好名声的\"。",
    "Greek doxa (opinion, repute) comes from dokein (to seem, to think), PIE *deḱ- (to take), kin to Latin decet, dignus, docere. Plato opposed doxa, opinion, to epistēmē, knowledge; ortho-doxos of right opinion (orthodox, a 4th-century Church word), hetero-doxos of other opinion, para-doxos contrary to expectation (paradox), doxo-logia a word of glory (doxology — doxa translated Hebrew kavod, glory, in Scripture), dogma what seems good — a decree, a tenet (dogmatic 1600s), Eudoxus of good repute."],
  aer: ["希腊语 aēr(下层空气、雾)来自 aēmi(吹),印欧语 *h₂weh₁-(吹),与拉丁语 ventus、英语 wind 同根;aithēr 是上层的\"燃烧的\"天空(ether)。罗马人借 aer 指空气(air 经法语 air,13 世纪);aerius 空中的(aerial 1600 年代),aero-nauta 是 1780 年代法语的\"空气水手\"——气球驾驶员,aero-plane 1866 年造(先指机翼),aero-dynamics 1837,aero-sol 1923,aerobic 是巴斯德 1863 年的\"需空气的\"(aérobie),malaria 是坏空气,aria 是意大利语的\"空气\"——咏叹调(\"曲调\"义来自\"气息\"),debonair 是 de bon aire(好出身)。",
    "Greek aēr (the lower air, mist) comes from aēmi (to blow), PIE *h₂weh₁- (to blow), kin to Latin ventus and English wind; aithēr was the upper, \"burning\" sky (ether). Rome borrowed aer for air (air through French, 13th century); aerius of the air (aerial 1600s), aero-nauta 1780s French for an air-sailor — a balloonist, aero-plane coined in 1866 (first for a wing), aero-dynamics 1837, aero-sol 1923, aerobic Pasteur's 1863 aérobie, needing air, malaria bad air, aria Italian for air — a melody (from breath), debonair de bon aire, of good stock."],
  electr: ["希腊语 ēlektron(琥珀)可能与 ēlektōr(闪耀的太阳)同源。古人知道摩擦琥珀能吸起草屑;1600 年吉尔伯特用新拉丁语 electricus(\"琥珀似的\")描述这种吸力,electricity 1640 年代,electron 是斯托尼 1891 年为电荷单位造的词(1897 年汤姆孙发现的粒子沿用),electrode 是法拉第 1834 年的\"电之路\",electrolysis 同年,electro-magnetic 1821,electrocute 是 1889 年美国仿 execute 造的,electronics 1910。所以\"电\"的本名是\"琥珀\"。",
    "Greek ēlektron (amber) may be kin to ēlektōr (the beaming sun). The ancients knew that rubbed amber picks up straw; in 1600 Gilbert used the New Latin electricus, \"amber-like\", for that attraction, electricity 1640s, electron Stoney's 1891 name for the unit of charge (kept for the particle Thomson found in 1897), electrode Faraday's 1834 \"electric way\", electrolysis the same year, electro-magnetic 1821, electrocute an 1889 American blend on execute, electronics 1910. Electricity is named after amber."],
  eth: ["希腊语 ēthos(习惯、性格)与 ethos(习俗)来自印欧语 *swe-dʰh₁-(自己的做法),与拉丁语 suescere(习惯)、consuetudo、英语 sib(?)同根。亚里士多德的《尼各马可伦理学》(Ēthika)是关于品格的学问,ēthikos 品格的——西塞罗译为 moralis;ethics 1400 年代,ethos 在修辞里是演说者的可信人格(1851 年借入英语),ethnos(民族——同习俗的人)是同根:ethnic 1375 年代先指\"异教的\",ethno-logy 1830 年代,ethno-graphy,ethology 是 1859 年穆勒的\"性格学\",20 世纪转为动物行为学。",
    "Greek ēthos (custom, character) and ethos (usage) come from PIE *swe-dʰh₁- (one's own way of doing), kin to Latin suescere (to be accustomed) and consuetudo. Aristotle's Nicomachean Ethics (Ēthika) is the study of character; ēthikos of character — Cicero rendered it moralis; ethics 1400s, ethos in rhetoric the speaker's credible character (borrowed into English 1851), ethnos (a people — those of one custom) is the same root: ethnic in the 1370s meant heathen, ethno-logy 1830s, ethno-graphy, ethology Mill's 1859 science of character, turned to animal behaviour in the 20th century."],
  petr: ["希腊语 petra(岩石)与 petros(石块)词源不明。耶稣给西门起名 Kēphas(阿拉米语\"石\"),希腊语译 Petros——\"你是彼得,我要在这磐石(petra)上建我的教会\"(《马太福音》16:18)是双关;Peter、Pierre、Pedro、Pietro 由此,petrificare 使成石(petrify 1590 年代),petr-oleum 是\"岩石油\"(中世纪拉丁语,1550 年代英语),petro-chemical 1913,petro-glyph 岩刻,saltpetre 是 sal petrae\"岩盐\"(硝石),parsley 是 petro-selinon\"岩芹\",petrology 岩石学。",
    "Greek petra (rock) and petros (a stone) are of unknown origin. Jesus named Simon Kēphas (Aramaic for stone), Greek Petros — \"you are Peter, and on this rock (petra) I will build my church\" (Matthew 16:18) is a pun; Peter, Pierre, Pedro, Pietro follow, petrificare to turn to stone (petrify 1590s), petr-oleum rock-oil (medieval Latin; English 1550s), petro-chemical 1913, petro-glyph a rock carving, saltpetre sal petrae, rock salt (nitre), parsley petro-selinon, rock-celery, petrology the study of rocks."],
  poe: ["希腊语 poiein(做、造)词源不明。poiētēs 是\"制作者\"——诗人(poet 经拉丁语 poeta),poiēma 是\"做出来的东西\"——诗(poem),poiēsis 制作——诗艺(poesy、poetry 经中世纪拉丁语 poetria),onomato-poiia 造名——拟声(onomatopoeia),pharmako-poiia 制药——药典(pharmacopoeia 1620 年代),poiētikē (technē) 是亚里士多德的《诗学》(Poetics),haemato-poiesis 造血。诗人在希腊语里是\"匠人\"。",
    "Greek poiein (to make, do) is of unknown origin. A poiētēs is a maker — a poet (through Latin poeta), a poiēma a thing made — a poem, poiēsis making — the art of verse (poesy, and poetry through medieval Latin poetria), onomato-poiia name-making — imitative words (onomatopoeia), pharmako-poiia drug-making — the pharmacopoeia (1620s), poiētikē (technē) Aristotle's Poetics, haemato-poiesis blood-making. In Greek the poet is a craftsman."],
  nat2: ["nasci(出生,分词 natus,古形 gnatus)来自印欧语 *ǵenh₁-,与 gignere、genus、英语 kin 同根——g 在拉丁语 n 前脱落。罗马人 natus 是\"生的\"(natus est 他出生了),natio 是\"一胎所生\"——部族(nation 从 13 世纪的\"出身相同的人群\"到 17 世纪的民族国家),natura 是\"生出的性质\"——自然(西塞罗译希腊语 physis),nativus 天生的(native、naive 是它经法语),natalis 出生的(Natale 意大利语圣诞——基督诞生日,Noël 法语,Natalie),prae-gnans 生前的——怀孕(pregnant),cognatus 同生的——血亲(cognate),agnatus 父系亲属,renaissance 重生,innate 天生,nascent 正在诞生的,puny 是 puis-né\"后生的\"。",
    "nasci (to be born, participle natus, older gnatus) comes from PIE *ǵenh₁-, kin to gignere, genus and English kin — Latin dropped the g before n. Roman natus is born (natus est, he was born), natio a brood — a tribe (nation went from a people of one birth in the 13th century to the nation-state in the 17th), natura the character one is born with — nature (Cicero's word for Greek physis), nativus inborn (native; naive is the same word through French), natalis of birth (Italian Natale, Christmas — Christ's birthday, French Noël, Natalie), prae-gnans before birth — pregnant, cognatus born together — blood kin (cognate), agnatus kin on the father's side, renaissance rebirth, innate inborn, nascent being born, puny is puis-né, born later."],
  cumul: ["cumulus(堆)来自印欧语 *ḱeuh₁-(胀、堆),同根有希腊语 kyein(怀孕)、kyma(波浪——胀起来的)、英语 hill?否,而是 cave 反义。罗马人 cumulus 是谷堆、也是\"顶点\";cumulare 堆积,ac-cumulare 堆在一起(accumulate 1520 年代),cumulativus 累积的(cumulative 1600 年代),cumulus 云是霍华德 1803 年的云分类——\"堆状云\",cumulonimbus 积雨云。",
    "cumulus (a heap) comes from PIE *ḱeuh₁- (to swell, heap up), shared with Greek kyein (to be pregnant) and kyma (a wave — a swelling). Roman cumulus was a heap of grain and the topmost point; cumulare to pile up, ac-cumulare to heap together (accumulate 1520s), cumulativus adding up (cumulative 1600s), cumulus the cloud in Luke Howard's classification of 1803 — the heaped cloud, cumulonimbus the heaped rain-cloud."],
  rar: ["rarus(稀疏、稀有)来自印欧语 *h₁erh₁-(分开)存疑,同根的可能有希腊语 erēmos(荒凉的——hermit 隐士)。罗马人 rarus 是织得疏的、树稀的、罕见的;raritas 稀有(rarity),rare-facere 使稀薄(rarefy;rarefied 稀薄的),rara avis 珍禽(尤维纳利斯),rare 1400 年代指稀有,肉的 rare(三分熟)是另一个词(古英语 hrere 半熟),rarely 很少。",
    "rarus (thin, sparse, rare) may come from PIE *h₁erh₁- (to separate), with Greek erēmos (desolate — hermit) as a possible cousin. Roman rarus was loosely woven, thinly wooded, seldom seen; raritas rarity, rare-facere to make thin (rarefy; rarefied), rara avis a rare bird (Juvenal), rare uncommon from the 1400s; rare of meat is another word (Old English hrere, lightly cooked), rarely."],
  trud: ["trudere(推)来自印欧语 *treud-(推、挤),同根有英语 threat(古义\"挤压\"——威胁)、thrust(经古挪威语)、哥特语 us-þriutan(压迫)。罗马人 trudere 推;in-trudere 推进去(intrude 1530 年代),ex-trudere 挤出(extrude;挤压成型),pro-trudere 向前推(protrude 1610 年代),ob-trudere 硬塞给人(obtrude、obtrusive 碍眼的),ab-strusus 是\"推开藏起的\"——深奥(abstruse),detrude 推下。",
    "trudere (to push) comes from PIE *treud- (to push, squeeze), shared with English threat (originally pressure) and thrust (via Old Norse), Gothic us-þriutan (to oppress). Romans trudere to shove; in-trudere to push in (intrude 1530s), ex-trudere to push out (extrude — of shaped metal), pro-trudere to push forward (protrude 1610s), ob-trudere to thrust upon (obtrude, obtrusive), ab-strusus pushed away and hidden — abstruse, detrude to push down."],
  tum: ["tumere(肿)来自印欧语 *teuh₂-(胀),同根有 tuber(块茎)、totus?(有争议)、希腊语 tymbos(坟丘——tomb)、英语 thumb(胖手指)、thigh、thousand(\"胀大的数\")、梵语 tumra(肥)。罗马人 tumor 肿胀(凯尔苏斯炎症四征之一;tumour 肿瘤义 15 世纪),tumidus 肿的(tumid 浮夸),tumulus 坟丘,tumultus 是\"膨胀的骚动\"——骚乱(tumult),con-tumax 傲慢的(contumacious),tuber 块根——truffle 是 tuber 经法语,protuberance 隆起,tumefy 使肿,thumb 是它的日耳曼亲戚。",
    "tumere (to swell) comes from PIE *teuh₂- (to swell), shared with tuber (a lump), Greek tymbos (a burial mound — tomb), English thumb (the fat finger), thigh, thousand (the swollen number), Sanskrit tumra (fat). Roman tumor is swelling (one of Celsus' four signs of inflammation; tumour as a growth from the 15th century), tumidus swollen (tumid, pompous), tumulus a burial mound, tumultus a swelling uproar (tumult), con-tumax insolent (contumacious), tuber a root-lump — truffle is tuber through French, protuberance a bulge, tumefy; thumb is its Germanic cousin."],
  vil: ["vilis(廉价、低贱)是拉丁语独有的词,词源不明。罗马人 vilis 是便宜的、不值钱的;vilitas 低廉,vili-ficare 贬低(vilify 1590 年代),vile 经法语 vil(14 世纪),re-vile 辱骂,vilipend 轻视,villain 与此无关(villa 的农民)——但两者在英语里互相影响,vileness。",
    "vilis (cheap, worthless) is a purely Latin word of unknown origin. Roman vilis is low-priced, of no account; vilitas cheapness, vili-ficare to make cheap (vilify 1590s), vile through French vil (14th century), re-vile to abuse, vilipend to despise; villain is unrelated (a villa's peasant), though the two coloured each other in English."],
  lab: ["拉丁语有两个 lab-。labor(劳动、辛苦)词源不明——可能与 labare(摇晃、承重)同源,\"在重负下摇晃\":罗马人 labor 是苦工、也是分娩(labour 至今两义),laborare 劳动(法语 travailler 用的是另一个词——tripalium 刑具),laboratorium 工作间(laboratory 1600 年代),e-laborare 精心做(elaborate),col-laborare 合作(collaborate 1870 年代),laborious 费力的。labi(滑)与分词 lapsus:lapsus 失误(lapse;lapsus linguae 口误),col-labi 一起倒(collapse),e-labi 溜走(elapse 时间流逝),re-labi 滑回(relapse 复发),labile 不稳定的,avalanche 是 ad-labina?存疑。",
    "Latin has two lab-. labor (toil) is of uncertain origin — perhaps kin to labare (to totter under a load): Roman labor was hard work and childbirth (labour keeps both), laborare to toil (French travailler uses another word, tripalium, an instrument of torture), laboratorium a workroom (laboratory 1600s), e-laborare to work out carefully (elaborate), col-laborare to work together (collaborate 1870s), laborious. labi (to slip) with participle lapsus: a lapsus a slip (lapse; lapsus linguae a slip of the tongue), col-labi to fall together (collapse), e-labi to slip away (elapse), re-labi to slide back (relapse), labile unstable."],
  proto: ["希腊语 prōtos(第一)是 pro(在前)的最高级,印欧语 *per-,与拉丁语 primus、英语 first 同根。prōto-typos 原型(prototype 1600 年代),prōto-kollon 是\"贴在卷首的页\"——记录卷宗内容的首页(protocol 从公证记录到外交礼仪,再到 1970 年代的网络协议),prōt-agōnistēs 第一个演员——主角,prōton 是卢瑟福 1920 年的\"第一个(粒子)\",prōteios 是穆尔德 1838 年的\"第一位的\"物质——蛋白质(protein),proto-plasm 1846,proto-zoa 最初的动物,Proterozoic 元古代,proto-language 原始语。",
    "Greek prōtos (first) is the superlative of pro (before), PIE *per-, kin to Latin primus and English first. prōto-typos the first mould (prototype 1600s), prōto-kollon the sheet glued to the front of a scroll — its table of contents (protocol went from notary's record to diplomatic etiquette to the network protocols of the 1970s), prōt-agōnistēs the first actor — the protagonist, proton Rutherford's 1920 \"first particle\", prōteios Mulder's 1838 \"of first rank\" substance — protein, proto-plasm 1846, proto-zoa the first animals, Proterozoic, proto-language."],
  un: ["unus(一)来自印欧语 *oi-no-(一),同根有希腊语 oinē(骰子的一点)、英语 one、an、any、德语 ein、俄语 odin。拉丁语 unus 给了 unio(联合——union 15 世纪,onion 是 unio 经法语 oignon——\"一个整体的\"洋葱!),unitas 统一(unity),unire 合一(unite),uni-versus 转成一体(universe;university 是 universitas——行会式的\"全体\"),uni-formis 一形(uniform),uni-cornis 一角(unicorn),uni-que 是法语的\"唯一\",unanimous 一心,unison 一声,null 是 ne-ullus\"没有一个\"——nullify、annul,once 与此无关(英语 one)。",
    "unus (one) comes from PIE *oi-no- (one), shared with Greek oinē (the ace on a die), English one, an, any, German ein, Russian odin. Latin unus gave unio (a joining — union 15th century; onion is unio through French oignon — the bulb that is one whole!), unitas oneness (unity), unire to make one (unite), uni-versus turned into one (universe; a university is a universitas, a guild-like whole), uni-formis of one shape (uniform), uni-cornis one-horned (unicorn), unique French for only one, unanimous of one mind, unison one sound, null is ne-ullus, not any one — nullify, annul."],
  neg: ["negare(否认)是 nec(不)加动词后缀——\"说不\";nec 来自印欧语 *ne(不),与 non、英语 no、not、never、德语 nein、梵语 na 同根。罗马人 negare 拒绝、否认;negatio 否定(negation),negativus 否定的(negative 1400 年代;照相负片 1841),ab-negare 拒绝给自己(abnegation 克己),re-negare 反复否认——背叛信仰(renegade 是西班牙语 renegado 叛教者,renege 赖账),neg-otium 是\"没有闲暇\"——事务、生意(negotiate 谈生意 1590 年代),neglegere 是 nec-legere 不去捡——忽视(neglect),nefas 不可说的——大逆(nefarious),neutral 是 ne-uter 两者都不。",
    "negare (to deny) is nec (not) with a verbal suffix — to say no; nec comes from PIE *ne (not), kin to non, English no, not, never, German nein, Sanskrit na. Romans negare to refuse, to deny; negatio denial (negation), negativus denying (negative 1400s; the photographic negative 1841), ab-negare to refuse oneself (abnegation), re-negare to deny again — to renounce one's faith (renegade is Spanish renegado, an apostate; renege), neg-otium \"no leisure\" — business (negotiate 1590s), neglegere is nec-legere, not to pick up — neglect, nefas the unspeakable — nefarious, neutral ne-uter, neither of the two."],
});
